// Rep Max questions: Computer Science (OCR H446). One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from data/spec.js), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 one-rep max),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// A line that isn't valid is skipped and listed at the bottom of the home screen. Lines starting // are ignored.
{"id":"cs-001","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"At the very start of the fetch stage of the fetch-decode-execute cycle, which transfer takes place?","o":["The contents of the MAR are copied to the PC","The instruction in the CIR is copied to the MDR","The contents of the PC are copied to the MAR","The contents of the ACC are copied to the MAR"],"a":2,"x":"Fetch begins by copying the address in the PC into the MAR. The memory contents at that address then arrive in the MDR, the PC is incremented, and the MDR is copied to the CIR.","w":["Backwards: the PC is copied to the MAR, not the MAR to the PC.","Backwards and too late: the MDR is copied to the CIR at the end of fetch.","","The ACC holds calculation results; it never supplies the address for a fetch."]}
{"id":"cs-002","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes Harvard architecture from Von Neumann architecture?","o":["Von Neumann has separate buses so an instruction and data can be fetched together","Harvard has separate memories and buses for instructions and for data","Harvard stores instructions and data together in one shared memory","Von Neumann has no registers, while Harvard has many"],"a":1,"x":"Harvard separates instruction and data memory (and buses), so both can be accessed at once. Von Neumann uses one shared memory and bus for both, which can cause a bottleneck.","w":["Swapped over: separate buses belong to Harvard, Von Neumann shares one.","","Swapped over: one shared memory for instructions and data is Von Neumann.","Both architectures use registers; the difference is in memory and buses."]}
{"id":"cs-003","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A processor has a 3-stage pipeline (fetch, decode, execute), each stage taking one clock cycle. With no branches, how many cycles are needed to finish 5 instructions?","o":["5","7","8","15"],"a":1,"x":"The first instruction takes 3 cycles. After that, one instruction finishes every cycle, so 3 + 4 = 7. Without pipelining it would take 5 × 3 = 15.","w":["Ignores filling the pipeline: the first instruction still takes 3 cycles.","","Used 3 + 5; after the first instruction only 4 remain, so 3 + 4 = 7.","15 is the unpipelined total, 5 × 3, with no overlap."]}
{"id":"cs-004","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The PC holds 12 and the ACC holds 6. Address 12 contains ADD 40 (direct addressing) and address 40 holds the value 9. What are the PC and ACC after one complete fetch-decode-execute cycle?","o":["PC = 13, ACC = 46","PC = 13, ACC = 15","PC = 12, ACC = 15","PC = 13, ACC = 9"],"a":1,"x":"The PC is incremented during fetch (12 to 13). ADD 40 adds the value stored at address 40 (9), not the number 40, to the ACC: 6 + 9 = 15.","w":["Added the address 40 itself; direct addressing uses the value stored there.","","Forgot the PC is incremented during fetch, so it is 13, not 12.","Treated ADD like a load: it adds 9 to the existing 6, not replaces it."]}
{"id":"cs-005","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which feature is typical of a RISC processor compared with a CISC processor?","o":["A large set of complex instructions of varying length","Fewer registers, relying on instructions that work on memory directly","More complex hardware needed to decode each instruction","A small set of simple instructions, most completing in one clock cycle"],"a":3,"x":"RISC uses a reduced set of simple, fixed-length instructions, often one cycle each, with more registers. CISC has many complex instructions that can take several cycles.","w":["That describes CISC, not RISC.","Backwards: RISC has more registers and mostly works on registers.","Complex decoding hardware is a CISC trait; RISC decoding is simpler.",""]}
{"id":"cs-006","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A task is split across the 4 cores of a processor but only runs about 2.5 times faster than on one core. What is the most likely reason?","o":["Each core runs at one quarter of the clock speed","Some parts must run in sequence, and cores wait for each other's results","The cores cannot share the same RAM","Parallel processing only works on a GPU"],"a":1,"x":"Parts of a program that depend on earlier results cannot run in parallel, and cores need time to share data. So the speed-up is less than the number of cores.","w":["Quarter-speed cores would give no speed-up at all, not 2.5 times.","","Cores in one processor do share RAM; that is how they cooperate.","Multi-core CPUs do parallel processing too; it is not GPU-only."]}
{"id":"cs-007","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about SSDs compared with magnetic hard disks is correct?","o":["They are cheaper per gigabyte","They have no moving parts, giving faster access and better shock resistance","They store data as pits and lands read by a laser","They use a read/write head moving over spinning platters"],"a":1,"x":"SSDs use flash memory with no moving parts, so they are quicker to access and tougher if dropped. Magnetic disks are usually cheaper per GB.","w":["SSDs usually cost more per GB than magnetic disks.","","Pits and lands read by a laser describe optical discs, not SSDs.","That describes a magnetic hard disk; SSDs have no moving parts."]}
{"id":"cs-008","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A laptop with 4 GB of RAM is asked to run programs needing 5 GB in total. What does the operating system do to cope?","o":["Moves the extra programs into ROM temporarily","Moves inactive pages from RAM to a swap area on secondary storage","Permanently compresses the programs so they fit in RAM","Pauses the CPU until enough RAM is free"],"a":1,"x":"This is virtual memory: pages not currently needed are swapped out to secondary storage and swapped back in when required. Secondary storage is much slower than RAM, so heavy swapping causes thrashing.","w":["ROM is read-only so cannot hold swapped programs.","","Programs are not permanently compressed; pages are swapped to disk.","The CPU is not paused; the OS swaps pages so work carries on."]}
{"id":"cs-009","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly contrasts segmentation with paging?","o":["Pages vary in size to suit each program; segments are all the same size","Segmentation only works on secondary storage; paging only works in RAM","Paging divides memory by logical meaning; segmentation does not","Segments are variable-sized logical units; pages are fixed-size blocks"],"a":3,"x":"Paging splits memory into equal, fixed-size pages with no regard to content. Segmentation splits it into variable-sized logical units, such as a procedure or a data table.","w":["Swapped over: pages are fixed-size, segments vary in size.","Both manage RAM and swap space; neither is limited to one kind of storage.","Backwards: segmentation follows logical units; paging ignores content.",""]}
{"id":"cs-010","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"At the end of a fetch-decode-execute cycle, the processor finds an interrupt with a higher priority than the current task. What does it do first?","o":["Restores the saved registers from the stack","Loads the start address of the interrupt service routine into the PC","Saves the current register contents, including the PC, onto a stack","Runs the interrupt service routine from its first instruction"],"a":2,"x":"The registers, including the PC, must be pushed onto a stack first, or the return address would be lost. Then the ISR's address is loaded into the PC and the ISR runs. Finally the registers are restored.","w":["Restoring registers happens last, after the ISR has finished.","Loading the ISR address comes after saving, or the return address is lost.","","Running the ISR comes only after registers are saved and its address is loaded."]}
{"id":"cs-011","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Round robin scheduling uses a 3 ms time slice. A needs 4 ms, B needs 2 ms and C needs 6 ms, all ready at time 0 in the order A, B, C. When does A finish?","o":["9 ms","4 ms","6 ms","12 ms"],"a":0,"x":"A runs 0-3 ms and joins the back of the queue, behind B and C. B runs 3-5 ms and finishes. C runs 5-8 ms and rejoins behind A. A runs 8-9 ms and finishes at 9 ms; C ends at 12 ms.","w":["","4 ms is A's burst time; it ignores waiting while B and C use their slices.","At 6 ms C is mid-slice; A's last 1 ms runs only from 8 to 9 ms.","12 ms is when C finishes, not A."]}
{"id":"cs-012","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which stage of compilation removes comments and white space and breaks the source code into tokens?","o":["Lexical analysis","Syntax analysis","Code generation","Optimisation"],"a":0,"x":"Lexical analysis turns source code into tokens such as keywords, identifiers and operators. Syntax analysis then checks the tokens follow the grammar of the language.","w":["","Syntax analysis comes next, checking tokens against the grammar rules.","Code generation produces the machine code near the end.","Optimisation makes code more efficient; it does not create tokens."]}
{"id":"cs-013","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage of dynamic linking (using a DLL) rather than static linking?","o":["The executable runs even if the library is missing","The executable file is larger and self-contained","No linker or loader is needed at all","Several programs can share one copy of the library in memory"],"a":3,"x":"With dynamic linking the library stays separate and is loaded when needed, so programs share it and it can be updated without recompiling. Static linking copies the library into every executable.","w":["Mixed up with static linking: if a DLL is missing, the program fails to run","Describes static linking; a dynamic executable is smaller as the library is separate","A linker and loader are still needed; the DLL is linked at load or run time",""]}
{"id":"cs-014","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which development methodology repeats a cycle in which risks are identified and analysed on every loop?","o":["Waterfall model","Rapid application development","Spiral model","Extreme programming"],"a":2,"x":"The spiral model goes round planning, risk analysis, development and evaluation, with risk analysis driving each loop. It suits large, high-risk projects.","w":["Waterfall runs through the stages once, with no repeated risk-analysis loop","RAD relies on prototyping and user feedback, not risk analysis on every loop","","XP uses short iterations and pair programming, not a risk-analysis loop"]}
{"id":"cs-015","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a feature of extreme programming?","o":["Pair programming, with two developers sharing one computer","A complete requirements document signed off before any coding","Risk analysis at the start of every spiral","Testing carried out only after all coding is complete"],"a":0,"x":"Extreme programming uses very short iterations, pair programming, test-first development and constant customer involvement. The other options describe waterfall or spiral.","w":["","That is waterfall; XP expects requirements to change as the project goes on","Risk analysis each loop belongs to the spiral model, not XP","XP is test-first, so tests are written before the code, not after"]}
{"id":"cs-016","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":3,"q":"The Little Man Computer program shown is run. What does it do?","code":"       INP\n       STA a\n       INP\n       STA b\n       SUB a\n       BRP bigger\n       LDA a\n       OUT\n       HLT\nbigger LDA b\n       OUT\n       HLT\na      DAT\nb      DAT","o":["Outputs the smaller of the two numbers entered","Outputs the larger of the two numbers entered","Outputs the difference between the two numbers","Outputs the sum of the two numbers"],"a":1,"x":"It stores the inputs as a and b, then computes b - a. If that is not negative (BRP), b is at least as large, so b is output. Otherwise a is output. Try 4 then 9, and 9 then 4: both give 9.","w":["Reversed: BRP branches when b − a ≥ 0, which outputs b, the larger","","The difference sits in ACC but is never output; OUT prints a or b","There is no ADD; SUB is only used to compare the two numbers"]}
{"id":"cs-017","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Location 30 holds 50 and location 50 holds 7. LDA 30 is executed using indirect addressing. What is loaded into the ACC?","o":["7","50","30","57"],"a":0,"x":"Indirect addressing treats the operand as the address of an address. Location 30 holds 50, so go to location 50 and load the value there: 7.","w":["","Stopped a step early: 50 is the address held in 30, not the final value","Took the operand itself as the value; indirect means following the address","Added 50 and 7; indirect addressing follows a pointer and does no arithmetic"]}
{"id":"cs-018","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"class Pet\n    private sound\n    public procedure new()\n        sound = \"...\"\n    endprocedure\n    public function speak()\n        return sound\n    endfunction\nendclass\nclass Dog inherits Pet\n    public procedure new()\n        super.new()\n    endprocedure\n    public function speak()\n        return \"Woof\" + super.speak()\n    endfunction\nendclass\nd = new Dog()\nprint(d.speak())","o":["...","Woof","...Woof","Woof..."],"a":3,"x":"d is a Dog, so Dog's speak overrides Pet's. It returns \"Woof\" joined to super.speak(), Pet's speak, which returns sound. sound was set to \"...\" when Dog's constructor called super.new(). Result: Woof...","w":["Ignores the override; Dog's speak adds \"Woof\" to the parent's result","Forgot that super.speak() returns sound, which is \"...\"","Joined in the wrong order: \"Woof\" comes first, then super.speak()",""]}
{"id":"cs-019","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about assembly language is correct?","o":["It runs unchanged on any processor","Each instruction usually translates to one machine code instruction","It is translated to machine code by an interpreter","Programs are written entirely as strings of 1s and 0s"],"a":1,"x":"Assembly uses mnemonics such as ADD and LDA, with an assembler translating roughly one-to-one into machine code. It is specific to a processor family, so it is not portable."}
{"id":"cs-020","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Run length encoding stores each run as an 8-bit count followed by an 8-bit character code. How many bits does it need to store AAAABBBCCDAA?","o":["64","96","80","40"],"a":2,"x":"The runs are AAAA, BBB, CC, D and AA: 5 runs, because the two A runs are not next to each other. 5 runs × 16 bits = 80 bits, against 96 bits uncompressed (12 characters × 8).","w":["Counted 4 runs by merging the two A runs; they are separate, so 5 runs","That is the uncompressed size, 12 × 8 bits","","Counted only 8 bits per run; each run needs a count and a character, 16 bits"]}
{"id":"cs-021","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this Caesar cipher program runs?","code":"msg = \"BYTE\"\nkey = 5\nout = \"\"\nfor i = 0 to msg.length - 1\n    n = ASC(msg.substring(i, 1)) - 65\n    out = out + CHR((n + key) MOD 26 + 65)\nnext i\nprint(out)","o":["G^YJ","GDYJ","FCXI","HEZK"],"a":1,"x":"Each letter shifts 5 places, wrapping round the alphabet: B to G, Y to D, T to Y, E to J. Without the MOD 26, Y would become the symbol ^.","w":["Forgot MOD 26 wrapping; Y goes round the alphabet to D, not ^","","Shifted by 4 instead of 5","Shifted by 6 instead of 5"]}
{"id":"cs-022","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Why do websites store a hash of each password rather than an encrypted copy?","o":["A hash cannot be reversed, so a stolen database does not reveal passwords","A hash can be decrypted with a key if the user forgets their password","Hashing produces a longer output, which is harder to guess","Hashing is reversible, so passwords can be emailed to users"],"a":0,"x":"Hashing is one-way: the site hashes what you type at login and compares it with the stored hash. Encrypted data could be decrypted if the key were stolen.","w":["","Hashes have no key and cannot be decrypted; forgotten passwords get reset instead","Output length is not the reason; the point is that hashing is one-way","Hashing is one-way, not reversible, so passwords cannot be emailed"]}
{"id":"cs-023","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"In a database, Orders.CustomerID is a foreign key referencing Customers. What does referential integrity prevent?","o":["Adding an order for a CustomerID that does not exist in Customers","Two customers sharing the same name","An order having more than one product","A customer having more than one order"],"a":0,"x":"Referential integrity means every foreign key value must match an existing primary key in the linked table. It stops orphan records such as an order for a non-existent customer.","w":["","Unique names need a separate constraint; a foreign key does not control that","Products per order is down to table design, not referential integrity","A foreign key allows one customer to have many orders"]}
{"id":"cs-024","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Table Pupil(PupilID, Name, Form, Score). Which query lists only the names of pupils in form 12B who scored over 60, highest score first?","o":["SELECT Name FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' OR Score > 60 ORDER BY Score DESC","SELECT Name FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' AND Score > 60 ORDER BY Score","SELECT * FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' AND Score > 60 ORDER BY Score DESC","SELECT Name FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' AND Score > 60 ORDER BY Score DESC"],"a":3,"x":"Both conditions are needed, so use AND. DESC puts the highest first (the default is ascending). SELECT Name returns only names, whereas SELECT * returns every field.","w":["OR lets in other forms or any score over 60; both conditions need AND","Missing DESC, so the lowest score comes first, not the highest","SELECT * returns every field, not just the names",""]}
{"id":"cs-025","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"OrderLine(OrderID, ProductID, ProductName, Quantity) has the composite key (OrderID, ProductID). ProductName depends only on ProductID. Which normal form does it fail first?","o":["Second normal form (2NF)","First normal form (1NF)","Third normal form (3NF)","None: it is already in 3NF"],"a":0,"x":"All values are atomic, so 1NF is met. ProductName depends on only part of the composite key (a partial dependency), which breaks 2NF. Fix it by moving ProductName into a Product table.","w":["","The values are atomic, so 1NF is met; the fault is a partial dependency","3NF is about non-key dependencies; the partial dependency breaks 2NF first","ProductName depends on part of the key, so it fails 2NF and so 3NF too"]}
{"id":"cs-026","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"A bank transfer takes £50 from account A, but the system crashes before crediting account B. The database undoes the first step. Which ACID property is this?","o":["Consistency","Isolation","Durability","Atomicity"],"a":3,"x":"Atomicity means a transaction happens completely or not at all. Rolling back the partial transfer stops money vanishing.","w":["Consistency keeps data rules valid; this is about all-or-nothing, not rules.","Isolation stops concurrent transactions interfering; only one transaction here.","Durability keeps committed data after a crash; this transaction never committed.",""]}
{"id":"cs-027","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which layer of the TCP/IP stack adds the source and destination IP addresses to each packet?","o":["Application layer","Transport layer","Internet (network) layer","Link layer"],"a":2,"x":"The Internet layer handles IP addressing and routing. The Transport layer (TCP) adds port numbers and sequence numbers, and the Link layer deals with MAC addresses on the local network.","w":["Application layer holds protocols like HTTP, not IP addresses.","Transport layer adds port and sequence numbers, not IP addresses.","","Link layer uses MAC addresses on the local network, not IP."]}
{"id":"cs-028","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about packet switching is correct?","o":["A dedicated path is reserved for the whole communication before sending","Packets may take different routes and are reassembled using sequence numbers","If packets arrive out of order the whole message is lost","Each packet carries a full copy of the message in case of loss"],"a":1,"x":"Packets are routed independently, so they can arrive out of order. The destination puts them back together using sequence numbers. Reserving a dedicated path is circuit switching.","w":["That describes circuit switching, not packet switching.","","Out-of-order packets are fine: sequence numbers let them be reassembled.","Each packet holds only part of the message plus a header."]}
{"id":"cs-029","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"What does a DNS server do when your browser asks about www.example.com?","o":["Returns the IP address that belongs to that domain name","Returns the web page for that domain","Encrypts the request before it is sent","Assigns your device a new IP address"],"a":0,"x":"DNS translates human-friendly domain names into IP addresses. The browser then uses the IP address to contact the web server. Assigning addresses to devices is DHCP.","w":["","DNS gives only the address; the web server sends the page.","Encryption is done by TLS/HTTPS, not by DNS.","Assigning IP addresses to devices is DHCP, not DNS."]}
{"id":"cs-030","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"What does a packet-filtering firewall do?","o":["Scans every file on the computer for known viruses","Encrypts all outgoing packets so they cannot be read","Checks each packet's addresses and ports against rules and allows or blocks it","Repeats lost packets until they are delivered"],"a":2,"x":"A firewall inspects packet headers (source and destination IP, port, protocol) against its rules. Virus scanning is done by anti-malware software.","w":["Virus scanning is anti-malware software, not a firewall's job.","Encrypting traffic is done by TLS or a VPN, not a packet filter.","","Resending lost packets is done by TCP, not a firewall."]}
{"id":"cs-031","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Which action must be done server-side rather than by JavaScript running in the browser?","o":["Checking a submitted password against the hashed copy in the database","Checking that an email field contains an @ symbol","Showing or hiding a menu when a button is clicked","Changing a button's colour when the mouse hovers over it"],"a":0,"x":"Anything involving secret or stored data, such as the user database, must run on the server because client-side code can be seen and changed by the user. The other tasks are fine in the browser.","w":["","Format checks like this can run in the browser (though the server should recheck).","Showing a menu changes the page only, so client-side JavaScript is fine.","A hover effect is purely visual, so it runs in the browser."]}
{"id":"cs-032","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which factor most increases a web page's PageRank?","o":["Repeating the search keyword many times on the page","Links to it from many other highly ranked pages","Being stored on a faster web server","Having a longer URL"],"a":1,"x":"PageRank counts links into a page, giving more weight to links from pages that are themselves highly ranked. Keyword stuffing and server speed are not part of the algorithm.","w":["Keyword stuffing is not part of PageRank, which counts links.","","Server speed does not affect PageRank.","URL length has no effect on PageRank."]}
{"id":"cs-033","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the binary number 10110110 in hexadecimal?","o":["6B","A6","B9","B6"],"a":3,"x":"Split into nibbles: 1011 = 11 = B and 0110 = 6. So 10110110 = B6.","w":["Nibbles swapped: 0110 1011 would be 6B, but the first nibble is 1011.","1010 is A, but the first nibble 1011 is B.","The second nibble 0110 is 6, not 9.",""]}
{"id":"cs-034","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"What is -45 in 8-bit two's complement?","o":["11010011","10101101","11010010","00101101"],"a":0,"x":"45 = 00101101. Invert the bits: 11010010. Add 1: 11010011. 10101101 is sign and magnitude, and 11010010 is only the one's complement.","w":["","Sign and magnitude: this only flips the sign bit, not two's complement.","Forgot to add 1 after inverting the bits (one's complement).","This is +45: nothing was inverted."]}
{"id":"cs-035","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Adding 01101100 and 01001110 as 8-bit two's complement numbers gives 10111010. Why is this result wrong?","o":["Underflow: the true sum is smaller than -128","A rounding error from converting to floating point","A parity error: the result has an odd number of 1s","Overflow: the true sum, 186, is bigger than the maximum of 127"],"a":3,"x":"108 + 78 = 186, but the largest 8-bit two's complement number is 127. The result spills into the sign bit, so 10111010 wrongly reads as -70.","w":["Both numbers are positive, so the sum is too big, not below -128.","No floating point is involved; this is integer addition.","Parity is for error checking and does not explain this result.",""]}
{"id":"cs-036","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"An 8-bit value x is stored in a register. Which operation sets bits 0 and 7 to 1 and leaves every other bit unchanged?","o":["x AND 10000001","x XOR 10000001","x OR 01111110","x OR 10000001"],"a":3,"x":"OR with a 1 forces a bit to 1, and OR with a 0 leaves it alone. AND would clear the other bits, and XOR would toggle bits 0 and 7 rather than set them.","w":["AND with 0 clears bits, so it wipes the middle bits and cannot set any.","XOR toggles, so a bit that is already 1 would flip to 0.","This sets bits 1 to 6 instead of bits 0 and 7.",""]}
{"id":"cs-037","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":3,"q":"A floating-point number has an 8-bit two's complement mantissa and a 4-bit two's complement exponent. Mantissa 00011010 and exponent 0101 are normalised. What is the new exponent?","o":["0011","0111","0100","0101"],"a":0,"x":"A positive normalised mantissa must start 01. Shift 00011010 left twice to get 01101000. Each left shift doubles the mantissa, so take 1 off the exponent per shift: 5 - 2 = 3 = 0011. The value stays 6.5.","w":["","Added 2 to the exponent; a left shift of the mantissa means taking away from it.","Only took 1 off; the mantissa needs two left shifts to start 01.","Kept the exponent, but two left shifts quadruple the mantissa, so it must drop by 2."]}
{"id":"cs-038","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"word = \"HAL\"\nout = \"\"\nfor i = 0 to word.length - 1\n    out = out + CHR(ASC(word.substring(i, 1)) + 1)\nnext i\nprint(out)","o":["GZK","IBM","HAL","JCN"],"a":1,"x":"ASC gives the code of each letter, 1 is added, and CHR converts back, so every letter moves one place on: H to I, A to B, L to M.","w":["Moved each letter back one place; the code adds 1, not subtracts.","","Ignored the +1 inside CHR(ASC(...)); the letters do change.","Added 2 rather than 1 to each letter's code."]}
{"id":"cs-039","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":1,"q":"A Stack class has methods push (adds an item to the top) and pop (removes and returns the top item). What is printed?","code":"s = new Stack()\ns.push(5)\ns.push(8)\ns.pop()\ns.push(3)\ns.push(6)\ns.pop()\nprint(s.pop())","o":["3","5","6","8"],"a":0,"x":"After the operations the stack goes [5], [5,8], [5], [5,3], [5,3,6], [5,3]. The final pop removes and returns the top item, 3.","w":["","Picked the bottom item; pop takes from the top of the stack, not the bottom.","6 was already removed by the earlier pop, so it is not the top now.","8 was popped early on; only the stack's final state matters."]}
{"id":"cs-040","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A circular queue is held in an array of size 5 (indices 0 to 4). The front pointer holds 3, the index of the first item, and the queue holds 4 items. At which index is the next item added?","o":["0","1","2","4"],"a":2,"x":"The items sit at indices 3, 4, 0 and 1, because the queue wraps round. The next free slot is (3 + 4) MOD 5 = 2.","w":["Index 0 already holds an item, because the queue wraps round to 0 and 1.","Index 1 is the last item in the queue, not the next free slot.","","Index 4 holds the second item; the free slot comes after 0 and 1, at 2."]}
{"id":"cs-041","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A hash table has indices 0 to 6 and uses key MOD 7 with linear probing (try the next slot, wrapping from 6 to 0). Keys 20, 34, 13, 9 and 27 are inserted in that order. Where is 27 stored?","o":["0","2","3","6"],"a":2,"x":"Home slots: 20, 34, 13 and 27 hash to 6; 9 hashes to 2. 20 takes 6, 34 wraps to 0, 13 goes to 1, 9 takes 2. 27 tries 6, 0, 1 and 2, all full, so it goes in 3.","w":["Index 0 is where 34 went after the clash at 6; 27 finds it full too.","Index 2 holds 9, so 27 has to probe on past it.","","Index 6 is 27's home slot, but 20 is already there."]}
{"id":"cs-042","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"The values 8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14, 4, 7 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. What is the parent of 7?","o":["3","4","8","6"],"a":3,"x":"7 < 8 so go left to 3. 7 > 3 so go right to 6. 7 > 6 and 6 has no right child, so 7 becomes the right child of 6.","w":["3 is an ancestor of 7, not its parent; 7 carries on down to 6.","4 is the left child of 6; 7 is bigger than 6 so goes right, not near 4.","8 is the root; 7 goes left but is placed lower down the tree.",""]}
{"id":"cs-043","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"According to De Morgan's law, NOT (A AND B) is equivalent to which expression?","o":["(NOT A) AND (NOT B)","NOT A AND B","(NOT A) OR (NOT B)","A OR B"],"a":2,"x":"De Morgan: negate each term and swap AND with OR. So NOT (A AND B) = (NOT A) OR (NOT B), and NOT (A OR B) = (NOT A) AND (NOT B).","w":["Negated both terms but forgot to swap AND for OR.","Only negated A; De Morgan needs both terms negated and the operator swapped.","","Dropped the NOTs altogether; this is not equivalent to the original."]}
{"id":"cs-044","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":3,"q":"Q is 1 only for (A, B, C) = 001, 011, 110 and 111. Which is the simplest expression for Q?","o":["(NOT A AND C) OR (B AND C)","(NOT A AND C) OR (A AND B)","(A AND B) OR C","B OR C"],"a":1,"x":"On a Karnaugh map, 001 and 011 group as NOT A AND C, and 110 and 111 group as A AND B. The other options give a 1 for an input that should be 0 (for example 101) or miss 110.","w":["(NOT A AND C) OR (B AND C) misses 110, where A AND B is needed.","","Gives 1 for 001, but also for 101, which should be 0.","Gives 1 for 010, which should be 0."]}
{"id":"cs-045","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"A full adder has inputs A = 1, B = 0 and carry in = 1. What are the sum and carry out?","o":["Sum = 1, carry out = 0","Sum = 1, carry out = 1","Sum = 0, carry out = 1","Sum = 0, carry out = 0"],"a":2,"x":"1 + 0 + 1 = 2, which is 10 in binary, so the sum bit is 0 and the carry out is 1.","w":["Ignored the carry in: 1 + 0 = 1, but 1 + 0 + 1 = 10, so sum 0, carry 1.","Sum would be 1 only if the total were odd; 1 + 0 + 1 = 2 gives sum 0.","","Sum 0 is right, but 1 + 0 + 1 = 10 in binary, so the 1 carries out."]}
{"id":"cs-046","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A rising-edge D-type flip-flop receives a rising clock edge with D = 1, so Q becomes 1. D then changes to 0 before the next rising edge. What is Q?","o":["Q becomes 0 immediately","Q stays at 1 until the next rising clock edge","Q becomes 0 when the clock next falls","Q becomes undefined"],"a":1,"x":"A D-type flip-flop only samples D on the clock edge. Between edges it holds its stored value, which is why it is used as a one-bit memory.","w":["Q does not follow D between edges; it only samples D on the clock edge.","","The falling edge does nothing here; this flip-flop only samples on rising edges.","Q is always defined in a D-type; it just holds the last sampled value."]}
{"id":"cs-047","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which action is an offence under the Computer Misuse Act 1990?","o":["Sharing a purchased film file with strangers online","Keeping customers' personal data for longer than necessary","Guessing a classmate's password to read their files without permission","Recording a friend's phone call with their agreement"],"a":2,"x":"Unauthorised access to computer material is the first offence in the Act, even if no damage is done. Sharing films is a copyright issue and retaining data is a Data Protection issue.","w":["Sharing a film is a copyright offence, not a Computer Misuse Act one.","Keeping data too long is a Data Protection issue, not Computer Misuse.","","Recording with agreement involves no unauthorised access to a computer system."]}
{"id":"cs-048","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A gym still holds the names, addresses and bank details of members who left 12 years ago, for no reason. Which Data Protection Act 2018 principle does this break?","o":["Data must be processed securely","Data must be accurate and kept up to date","Data must be kept no longer than necessary","Data must only be used for the purpose it was collected"],"a":2,"x":"This is the storage limitation principle: personal data should only be kept as long as there is a lawful reason to hold it.","w":["Security is about protecting data from breaches; the issue here is how long it is held.","Accuracy is about correcting wrong data; these records are old, not necessarily wrong.","","Purpose limitation is about how data is used, but this is about how long it is kept."]}
{"id":"cs-049","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A hiring algorithm trained on 20 years of a company's past decisions rejects most female applicants, because the company mainly hired men. Which issue is this?","o":["The digital divide","Censorship","Algorithmic bias","Net neutrality"],"a":2,"x":"Machine learning copies patterns in its training data, including unfair ones. Biased data leads to biased decisions, which raises ethical and legal concerns.","w":["Digital divide is about unequal access to technology, not unfair decisions from data.","Censorship means restricting content, which has nothing to do with biased training data.","","Net neutrality is about ISPs treating all traffic equally, not about unfair algorithm output."]}
{"id":"cs-050","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A London Underground map shows stations and lines but ignores real distances and the streets above. Which computational thinking technique does this show?","o":["Abstraction","Decomposition","Backtracking","Concurrency"],"a":0,"x":"Abstraction removes unnecessary detail so that only what matters (the connections) remains.","w":["","Decomposition splits a problem into smaller parts; the map hides detail instead.","Backtracking is trying options and undoing them; it is not about hiding detail.","Concurrency is running tasks at the same time; it does not describe a simplified map."]}
{"id":"cs-051","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A function calculates the average of the numbers in an array. Which is a sensible precondition for it?","o":["The function must use a loop","The result must be a whole number","The array must contain at least one number","The average must not exceed 100"],"a":2,"x":"A precondition states what must be true before the subroutine runs. With an empty array the function would divide by zero.","w":["A loop is an implementation choice, not a condition that must hold before the call.","This is about the output (a postcondition), and averages are often not whole numbers.","","This limits the result, not the input, and an average can legitimately exceed 100."]}
{"id":"cs-052","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"function step(n)\n    if n MOD 2 == 0 then\n        return n DIV 2\n    endif\n    return 3 * n + 1\nendfunction\nprint(step(step(step(6))))","o":["10","5","16","3"],"a":1,"x":"step(6) = 3 (even, so halve). step(3) = 10 (odd, so 3n + 1). step(10) = 5 (even, so halve). The innermost call runs first, so the answer is 5.","w":["Stopped after two calls; step(step(6)) is 10, but there is a third call.","","Went one call too far: 16 is step(5), which would be a fourth call.","Only one call done: step(6) is 3, but it is then passed into step twice more."]}
{"id":"cs-053","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"How many lines of output does this program produce?","code":"for i = 1 to 15\n    if i MOD 3 == 0 AND i MOD 5 == 0 then\n        print(\"FB\")\n    elseif i MOD 3 == 0 then\n        print(\"F\")\n    elseif i MOD 5 == 0 then\n        print(\"B\")\n    endif\nnext i","o":["8","7","5","3"],"a":1,"x":"3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 12 and 15 each print one line. 15 matches the first condition, so it prints only once. Adding 5 + 3 gives 8, but 15 was counted twice.","w":["Added 5 + 3, but 15 is in both lists and prints only once, so it was counted twice.","","Counted only the multiples of 3 (3, 6, 9, 12, 15) and forgot the multiples of 5.","Counted only the multiples of 5 (5, 10, 15) and forgot the multiples of 3."]}
{"id":"cs-054","s":"cs","t":"2.1.5","lv":2,"q":"Two processes each read a shared balance of 100, add 50, and write the result back. Both read before either writes. What is the final balance?","o":["200","100","250","150"],"a":3,"x":"Both processes calculate 100 + 50 = 150 and write 150, so one update is lost instead of reaching 200. This is a race condition, and it is why shared data needs locking.","w":["Assumes both updates apply, but each process wrote 150 over the other's result.","Each process does add 50 before writing, so the balance cannot stay at 100.","Both processes add only 50 to the 100 they read, so the total is not 250.",""]}
{"id":"cs-055","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"total = 0\nfor i = 1 to 10 step 3\n    total = total + i\nnext i\nprint(total)","o":["22","12","35","55"],"a":0,"x":"The loop variable takes the values 1, 4, 7, 10 (the end value is inclusive). 1 + 4 + 7 + 10 = 22.","w":["","Left out 10; the end value of a for loop is inclusive, so 10 is added.","Went past the end value; the loop stops after i = 10 and does not reach 13.","This is 1 to 10 added with step ignored; step 3 gives 1, 4, 7, 10."]}
{"id":"cs-056","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"The function f is called as f(5). Including that first call, how many times is f called in total?","code":"function f(n)\n    if n <= 1 then\n        return 1\n    endif\n    return f(n - 1) + f(n - 2)\nendfunction\nprint(f(5))","o":["8","9","15","11"],"a":2,"x":"Calls for f(n) = 1 + calls(n-1) + calls(n-2). Starting from 1 call each for f(0) and f(1), the totals are 3, 5, 9 and 15 for n = 2, 3, 4, 5. f(5) returns 8, a common trap.","w":["This is the value f(5) returns, not the number of calls made.","This is the number of calls for f(4), one n too low.","","Miscounted the call tree; with calls(n) = 1 + calls(n-1) + calls(n-2) it has 15 calls."]}
{"id":"cs-057","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"procedure bump(a:byVal, b:byRef)\n    a = a + 10\n    b = b + 10\nendprocedure\n\nx = 1\ny = 1\nbump(x, y)\nprint(str(x) + \",\" + str(y))","o":["1,1","11,11","11,1","1,11"],"a":3,"x":"a is passed by value, so the procedure changes only a copy and x stays 1. b is passed by reference, so y itself becomes 11.","w":["y is passed by reference, so the change to b does change y.","a is passed by value, so the change to a does not affect x.","Swapped the two: the by-value x stays 1 and the by-reference y changes.",""]}
{"id":"cs-058","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? Parameters are passed by value unless byRef is stated.","code":"global total = 0\n\nprocedure add(n)\n    total = total + n\n    n = 100\nendprocedure\n\nn = 5\nadd(n)\nadd(n)\nprint(total + n)","o":["205","110","15","10"],"a":2,"x":"total is global, so both calls add 5 to it: total = 10. The parameter n is passed by value, so setting it to 100 inside the procedure does not change the main n, which stays 5. 10 + 5 = 15.","w":["Let n become 100 after the first call; passed by value, the main n stays 5.","Added 100 to n at the end; by value, the main n stays 5, not 100.","","Forgot the final + n: total is 10, but the print shows total + n = 15."]}
{"id":"cs-059","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about this program is correct?","code":"array scores[5]\nscores[0] = 7\nscores[1] = 3\nscores[2] = 9\nscores[3] = 4\nscores[4] = 6\ntotal = 0\nfor i = 0 to 5\n    total = total + scores[i]\nnext i","o":["The loop runs 5 times and total finishes as 29","The loop stops at i = 4, because 'to 5' excludes 5","The program is fine, but total is 35 because i is added too","The loop runs 6 times, so scores[5] is outside the array and causes an error"],"a":3,"x":"A for loop includes its end value, so i goes 0 to 5 (6 times). The array has indices 0 to 4, so scores[5] is out of range. The fix is for i = 0 to 4.","w":["A for loop includes its end value, so it runs 6 times, not 5.","Wrong: the end value of a for loop is inclusive, so i does reach 5.","The loop adds scores[i], not i itself, and it also goes out of range.",""]}
{"id":"cs-060","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":3,"q":"The function power is called as power(2, 10). Including that first call, how many times is power called in total?","code":"function power(b, e)\n    if e == 0 then\n        return 1\n    endif\n    half = power(b, e DIV 2)\n    if e MOD 2 == 0 then\n        return half * half\n    endif\n    return half * half * b\nendfunction\nprint(power(2, 10))","o":["5","4","6","10"],"a":0,"x":"The exponent halves each time: 10, 5, 2, 1, 0. That is 5 calls. Halving the problem each time is divide and conquer.","w":["","Missed the base case call: the exponents are 10, 5, 2, 1 and 0, which is 5 calls.","Counted a call after e = 0, but the base case returns without calling again.","Assumed one call per unit of the exponent, but halving gives far fewer calls."]}
{"id":"cs-061","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"What is a heuristic?","o":["A method that always finds the best solution, however slowly","A way of splitting a problem into two halves","A rule of thumb that finds a good solution quickly, but not always the best one","A routine that undoes the last step when it hits a dead end"],"a":2,"x":"Heuristics trade perfect accuracy for speed, for example estimating the distance to the goal in A*. Undoing steps is backtracking, and halving problems is divide and conquer.","w":["That describes an exhaustive or optimal algorithm; a heuristic gives up accuracy for speed","That is divide and conquer, not a heuristic","","That is backtracking, which is not what a heuristic is"]}
{"id":"cs-062","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"What is the time complexity of this loop in terms of n?","code":"i = 1\nwhile i < n\n    i = i * 2\nendwhile","o":["O(log n)","O(n)","O(n log n)","O(2^n)"],"a":0,"x":"i doubles each time (1, 2, 4, 8, ...), so it reaches n after about log2(n) steps. For n = 1024 it runs just 10 times.","w":["","Thinks the loop adds 1 each time; i doubles, so it takes far fewer than n steps","Mixes this up with merge sort; there is only one loop with no inner work","i grows like $2^k$, so the step count is about $\\log_2 n$, not $2^n$"]}
{"id":"cs-063","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A bubble sort is used to sort 6 3 5 1 4 into ascending order. What does the list look like after the first complete pass?","o":["3 5 6 1 4","3 1 4 5 6","1 3 4 5 6","3 5 1 4 6"],"a":3,"x":"Compare neighbours and swap if needed: 6 bubbles all the way along, giving 3 5 1 4 6. The largest value is now in place. More passes are needed.","w":["Stopped the pass early; 6 keeps swapping all the way to the end","Too much sorting for one pass; 1 can only move one place left per pass","Fully sorted; that needs several passes, not just the first",""]}
{"id":"cs-064","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":3,"q":"How many lines of output does this program produce?","code":"low = 1\nhigh = 100\ndo\n    guess = (low + high) DIV 2\n    print(guess)\n    if guess < 90 then\n        low = guess + 1\n    else\n        high = guess - 1\n    endif\nuntil guess == 90","o":["5","6","8","7"],"a":3,"x":"The guesses printed are 50, 75, 88, 94, 91, 89 and 90. Each guess halves the remaining range (binary search), and the loop stops when guess is 90.","w":["Stops at 91; the range still narrows to 89 and then 90 is printed","Missed one; there are 7 guesses: 50, 75, 88, 94, 91, 89, 90","Counted one too many; the loop ends as soon as 90 is printed",""]}
{"id":"cs-065","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A binary tree has root 8. Node 3 is its left child, with children 1 and 6. Node 10 is its right child, with right child 14. Which is the post-order traversal?","o":["1, 6, 3, 14, 10, 8","8, 3, 1, 6, 10, 14","1, 3, 6, 8, 10, 14","8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14"],"a":0,"x":"Post-order visits left subtree, right subtree, then the root. The other options are pre-order, in-order and breadth-first.","w":["","That is pre-order: root first, then left, then right","That is in-order: left, root, right","That is breadth-first: level by level"]}
{"id":"cs-066","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":3,"q":"An undirected graph has edges A-B 7, A-C 3, B-C 2, B-D 4, C-D 8, C-E 9, D-E 1. What is the shortest distance from A to E, as found by Dijkstra's algorithm?","o":["9","11","12","10"],"a":3,"x":"Dijkstra finalises A (0), C (3), B (5), D (9), then E (10). The route is A-C-B-D-E: 3 + 2 + 4 + 1 = 10. Direct routes such as A-C-E cost 12.","w":["That is only the shortest distance to D, not to E","No route to E costs 11; 11 is A-C-D, which only reaches D","Took the direct-looking A-C-E (3 + 9) without checking the route via B and D",""]}
{"id":"cs-067","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A* expands the open node with the lowest f = g + h. The open nodes are P (g=6, h=4), Q (g=3, h=9), R (g=5, h=3) and S (g=2, h=7). Which is expanded next?","o":["P","Q","S","R"],"a":3,"x":"f values: P = 10, Q = 12, R = 8, S = 9. R has the lowest, so it is expanded next. Dijkstra would use g alone and pick S.","w":["Looked at the largest g only; A* needs g + h, and P's is 10","Picked the largest h; Q's f is 12, the highest, not the lowest","Used g alone, which is Dijkstra; S's f is 9, above R's 8",""]}
{"id":"cs-068","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the main role of the control unit in the processor?","o":["It decodes each instruction and sends out control signals","It carries out arithmetic and logical operations on data","It holds the address of the next instruction to fetch","It stores the result of the most recent calculation"],"a":0,"x":"The control unit decodes each instruction and sends control signals to coordinate the ALU, registers, memory and buses. Arithmetic is the ALU's job, the next address is in the PC, and results are held in the ACC.","w":["","That is the ALU's job, not the control unit's","That is the program counter, not the control unit","That is the accumulator, not the control unit"]}
{"id":"cs-069","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the buses in a Von Neumann computer is correct?","o":["The data bus is one-way, carrying data only from memory to the processor","The address bus is one-way, carrying addresses from the processor to memory","The address bus carries the data values that are read from memory","The control bus carries only the data values being written to memory"],"a":1,"x":"Addresses are always sent out by the processor, so the address bus is one-way. The data bus is two-way (reads and writes). The control bus carries signals such as read, write and clock, not data.","w":["The data bus is two-way; it carries data for both reads and writes","","The address bus carries addresses, not data values","The control bus carries signals like read and write, not data"]}
{"id":"cs-070","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Why does a larger cache usually make a processor perform better?","o":["It raises the clock speed, so each cycle takes less time","It adds extra cores, so more instructions can run in parallel","It holds more of the data in use, so slow RAM is read less often","It widens the data bus, so more bits move in each transfer"],"a":2,"x":"Cache is small, fast memory next to the processor. A bigger cache holds more of the data and instructions in use, so the processor waits for slower main memory less often. Clock speed, cores and bus width are separate factors.","w":["Cache size does not change clock speed; that is set separately","Cores are separate hardware; more cache does not add any","","Bus width is a separate feature; cache does not widen it"]}
{"id":"cs-071","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A pipelined processor fetches the instructions after a branch while the branch is still executing. The branch is taken. What happens to those instructions?","o":["They are executed anyway and their results are kept","They wait in the pipeline until the branch target has finished","They are copied into cache so they can be reused later","They are discarded, because the program jumps somewhere else"],"a":3,"x":"The early fetches assumed the program carried on in order. When the branch jumps elsewhere, they are the wrong instructions, so the pipeline is flushed and fetching restarts at the target. This is why branches slow pipelines.","w":["They were fetched for the wrong path, so their results must not be kept","They do not wait; they are the wrong instructions and are thrown away","Cache is not used to store them; they are simply flushed",""]}
{"id":"cs-073","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the purpose of a device driver?","o":["It speeds up the device by keeping a copy of its data in cache","It converts general OS commands into ones a particular device understands","It scans the device for malicious software before use","It decides how much RAM each connected device may use"],"a":1,"x":"Each device understands its own set of commands. The driver acts as a translator between the operating system and that specific hardware, so the OS does not need to know the details of every device.","w":["That describes caching, not what a driver does","","That is anti-malware software, not a driver","RAM allocation is memory management, done by the OS itself"]}
{"id":"cs-074","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which describes the role of the BIOS when a computer is switched on?","o":["It loads every installed application into RAM","It moves pages between RAM and the hard disk as memory fills","It tests the hardware, then starts loading the operating system","It decides which process the processor will run first"],"a":2,"x":"The BIOS is stored in non-volatile memory. At start-up it runs a power-on self test of the hardware and then starts the bootstrap loader, which loads the operating system. Paging and scheduling are done by the OS after that.","w":["Mixes up BIOS with the OS: applications only load when you open them","That is paging, which the OS does once running, not the BIOS","",""]}
{"id":"cs-075","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A computer with very little RAM spends most of its time moving pages between RAM and disk, and little useful work gets done. What is this called?","o":["Deadlock","Fragmentation","Pre-emption","Thrashing"],"a":3,"x":"Thrashing happens when RAM is so short that pages are swapped in and out constantly. Deadlock is processes waiting for each other's resources. Fragmentation is wasted or scattered memory, not constant swapping.","w":["Deadlock is processes waiting on each other's resources, not constant swapping","Fragmentation is memory split into unusable gaps, not constant page swapping","Pre-emption is the scheduler interrupting a process, not swapping pages",""]}
{"id":"cs-076","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Jobs of length 7, 3, 2 and 4 ms all arrive at time 0 in that order. With shortest job first and no pre-emption, what is the average waiting time?","o":["4 ms","7.25 ms","8 ms","16 ms"],"a":0,"x":"SJF runs 2, 3, 4, 7. Waiting times are 0, 2, 5 and 9, a total of 16, so the average is 16 / 4 = 4 ms. 7.25 is first come first served, 8 is average turnaround time, and 16 forgets to divide.","w":["","7.25 ms is the first come first served average, not shortest job first","8 ms is the average turnaround time, which includes each job's run time","Total waiting time is 16 ms; you forgot to divide by 4 jobs"]}
{"id":"cs-077","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Shortest remaining time (pre-emptive): P1 arrives at 0 needing 6 ms, P2 arrives at 2 needing 3 ms, P3 arrives at 3 needing 1 ms. At what time does P2 finish?","o":["5 ms","6 ms","9 ms","10 ms"],"a":1,"x":"P1 runs 0-2 (4 left). P2 (3) pre-empts P1 at 2. At 3, P3 (1) pre-empts P2 (2 left), running 3-4. P2 then resumes and finishes at 6. 5 forgets P3, 9 is first come first served, 10 is non-pre-emptive SJF.","w":["Forgets P3 pre-empting P2 for 1 ms, so P2 finishes later than 5","","9 ms is first come first served order, but this scheduling is pre-emptive","10 ms is non-pre-emptive order: P1, P3, then P2, so it ignores pre-emption"]}
{"id":"cs-078","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"Why is Unicode used instead of ASCII to store text in many languages?","o":["It stores every character in fewer bits, saving space","It stores characters in a form that is faster to compare","It has far more code points, so it covers many languages and symbols","It can only encode letters, so the character set is simpler"],"a":2,"x":"Standard ASCII uses 7 bits and has only 128 characters, enough for English text. Unicode has many thousands of code points covering world languages and symbols. It generally needs more bits per character, not fewer.","w":["Unicode usually needs more bits per character, not fewer","Speed of comparison is not the reason; the point is the much larger character set","","Unicode encodes letters, digits, symbols and emoji, not just letters"]}
{"id":"cs-079","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"a = \"7\"\nb = \"5\"\nprint(a + b)\nprint(int(a) + int(b))","o":["12, then 12","12, then 75","75, then 75","75, then 12"],"a":3,"x":"a and b are strings, so + joins them: \"75\". int() casts each string to an integer first, so the second + is addition: 12. The trap is thinking + always adds.","w":["Assumes + adds the strings; with strings it joins them to give 75","Reversed: the strings join to 75 first, and the int() line adds to 12","The second line casts with int(), so it adds to 12 rather than joining",""]}
{"id":"cs-080","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which logic gate produces the carry output of a half adder?","o":["AND","XOR","OR","NAND"],"a":0,"x":"A carry is produced only when both inputs are 1, which is the AND of the two bits. The sum output is the XOR of the inputs.","w":["","XOR gives the sum output of a half adder, not the carry","OR is 1 if either input is 1, but a carry needs both inputs to be 1","NAND is 0 when both inputs are 1, the opposite of a carry"]}
{"id":"cs-081","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which expression is equivalent to A OR (A AND B)?","o":["B","A","A AND B","A OR B"],"a":1,"x":"This is the absorption law. If A is 1 the whole expression is 1, and if A is 0 then A AND B is also 0. So the result always equals A, whatever B is.","w":["B ignores A; if A is 1 the whole expression is 1 whatever B is","","A AND B is only part of it; A = 1, B = 0 gives 1, not 0","A OR B gives 1 when A = 0, B = 1, but the original gives 0 there"]}
{"id":"cs-082","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"Q is 1 only for (A, B, C) = 000, 001, 100 and 101. Which is the simplest expression for Q?","o":["B","NOT A AND NOT B","NOT B","NOT B AND NOT C"],"a":2,"x":"On a Karnaugh map the four 1s form one group of four, in which B = 0 and A and C take every value. A and C drop out, leaving NOT B. NOT A AND NOT B covers only 000 and 001.","w":["Backwards: Q is 1 when B = 0, not when B = 1","NOT A AND NOT B covers only 000 and 001, missing 100 and 101","","NOT B AND NOT C misses 001 and 101; C does not matter in the group"]}
{"id":"cs-083","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A circuit outputs Q = (NOT (A AND B)) AND (A OR B). Which single gate behaves in the same way?","o":["XNOR","NAND","NOR","XOR"],"a":3,"x":"NOT (A AND B) is 0 only for 11, and A OR B is 0 only for 00. Q is 1 exactly when the inputs differ, which is XOR. XNOR is the opposite; NAND and NOR give 1 for 00.","w":["XNOR is the opposite: it is 1 when inputs match, but Q is 0 for 00 and 11","NAND gives 1 for 00, but Q is 0 there because A OR B is 0","NOR gives 1 for 00 and 0 for 01, but Q is 0 for 00 and 1 for 01",""]}
{"id":"cs-085","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? Positions start at 0, and substring(start, numberOfChars) takes numberOfChars characters from position start.","code":"word = \"COMPUTER\"\nprint(word.substring(2, 3))\nprint(word.length)","o":["OMP, then 8","MPU, then 8","PUT, then 8","MPU, then 7"],"a":1,"x":"Position 2 of COMPUTER is M, and 3 characters give MPU. length counts all the characters, which is 8, not the last position (7).","w":["Starts at position 1 as if counting from 1; positions start at 0","","Starts one place too late; position 2 is M, not P","Substring is right, but length counts all 8 characters; 7 is the last position"]}
{"id":"cs-086","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? Parameters are passed by value unless byRef is stated.","code":"procedure swap(a:byVal, b:byRef)\n    t = a\n    a = b\n    b = t\nendprocedure\n\nx = 3\ny = 8\nswap(x, y)\nprint(x * 10 + y)","o":["83","38","33","88"],"a":2,"x":"a is a copy of x, so changing a leaves x as 3. b is y itself. a = b makes a 8, then b = t sets y to 3. The result is 33. 83 wrongly treats both as byRef.","w":["Treats both as byRef; a is byVal, so x stays 3","Assumes nothing changes, but byRef b sets y to 3","","Assumes x becomes 8, but a is a copy and y is what changes, to 3"]}
{"id":"cs-087","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"n = 4821\ntotal = 0\nwhile n > 0\n    total = total + (n MOD 10)\n    n = n DIV 10\nendwhile\nprint(total)","o":["14","534","1","15"],"a":3,"x":"Each pass adds the last digit (n MOD 10), then removes it (n DIV 10): 1 + 2 + 8 + 4 = 15. The loop stops when n reaches 0, so every digit is included. 534 adds n DIV 10 instead.","w":["Misses the first digit taken, 1; all four digits 1 + 2 + 8 + 4 make 15","Adds n DIV 10 (the number left over) instead of the last digit n MOD 10","Only takes the last digit, 1, and ignores that the loop repeats for every digit",""]}
{"id":"cs-088","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? calls is a global variable that rev can change.","code":"global calls = 0\n\nfunction rev(s)\n    calls = calls + 1\n    if s.length <= 1 then\n        return s\n    endif\n    return rev(s.substring(1, s.length - 1)) + s.substring(0, 1)\nendfunction\n\nprint(rev(\"STACK\"))\nprint(calls)","o":["KCATS, 5","STACK, 5","KCATS, 4","KCATS, 6"],"a":0,"x":"Each call moves the first letter to the end, so STACK becomes KCATS. rev is called on STACK, TACK, ACK, CK and K: 5 calls. K has length 1, so it stops.","w":["","Each call puts the first letter after the rest, so the word comes out reversed.","Misses a call: rev runs on the 1-letter string K too, so 5 calls, not 4.","Counts an extra call on an empty string; the recursion stops at K, so 5 calls."]}
{"id":"cs-089","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A list of 500,000 unsorted names is searched once for a single name. Which method is best?","o":["Binary search, because it always needs fewer comparisons","Binary search, because it also works on unsorted lists","Linear search, because sorting first would cost more than one search","Linear search, because it has a time complexity of O(log n)"],"a":2,"x":"Binary search needs a sorted list, and sorting 500,000 items takes far longer than one linear pass. Binary search is faster only when the list is already sorted or is searched many times.","w":["Binary search is only faster on sorted data; sorting 500,000 names first costs more.","Binary search needs a sorted list; it fails on unsorted data.","","Linear search is O(n), not O(log n); the right reason is the cost of sorting."]}
{"id":"cs-090","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"How many times does the while loop body run when this program runs?","code":"array data[8]\ndata[0] = 3\ndata[1] = 8\ndata[2] = 12\ndata[3] = 17\ndata[4] = 21\ndata[5] = 25\ndata[6] = 30\ndata[7] = 34\ntarget = 34\nlow = 0\nhigh = 7\ncount = 0\nwhile low <= high\n    mid = (low + high) DIV 2\n    count = count + 1\n    if data[mid] == target then\n        low = high + 1\n    elseif data[mid] < target then\n        low = mid + 1\n    else\n        high = mid - 1\n    endif\nendwhile\nprint(count)","o":["3","7","8","4"],"a":3,"x":"The midpoints are index 3, 5, 6 and 7, so 34 is found on the 4th pass. A linear search would need 8 passes. The search area roughly halves each time.","w":["Stops counting early: the midpoints are 3, 5, 6 and 7, so there are 4 passes, not 3.","Confuses the index of the target (7) with the number of passes.","8 is the number of passes for a linear search; binary search halves the range each time.",""]}
{"id":"cs-091","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"After this code runs, what are the contents of array a, in order from a[0] to a[4]?","code":"array a[5]\na[0] = 5\na[1] = 2\na[2] = 9\na[3] = 1\na[4] = 6\nfor i = 1 to 3\n    temp = a[i]\n    j = i - 1\n    placed = false\n    while j >= 0 AND placed == false\n        if a[j] > temp then\n            a[j + 1] = a[j]\n            j = j - 1\n        else\n            placed = true\n        endif\n    endwhile\n    a[j + 1] = temp\nnext i","o":["2, 5, 9, 1, 6","1, 2, 5, 9, 6","1, 2, 5, 6, 9","1, 5, 2, 9, 6"],"a":1,"x":"Insertion sort, but the for loop stops at i = 3, so a[4] is never inserted. After i = 2 the array is 2, 5, 9, 1, 6. At i = 3 the 1 moves to the front, giving 1, 2, 5, 9, 6.","w":["Stops after i = 2: the loop runs for i = 3 as well, so the 1 moves to the front.","","Assumes a full sort, but the loop stops at i = 3 so a[4] is never inserted.","Skips the i = 1 pass: the 2 is inserted before the 5 first, giving 1, 2, 5, 9, 6."]}
{"id":"cs-092","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which task is best suited to being run on a GPU rather than on a single CPU core?","o":["Running a sequence of calculations where each step needs the previous result","Handling keyboard interrupts and managing the file system","Applying the same brightness change to each of millions of pixels in an image","Deciding which process gets the next slice of processor time"],"a":2,"x":"GPUs have hundreds or thousands of simple cores that run the same operation on lots of independent data at once. Pixel changes are independent. Sequential, dependent or decision-heavy tasks suit the CPU.","w":["Each step depends on the last, so it cannot be spread across many cores; this is the sequential trap.","Interrupts and file management are general control tasks for the CPU and the operating system.","","Scheduling is a decision-making job for the CPU, not a large batch of identical calculations."]}
{"id":"cs-093","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"The same program is compiled for a RISC processor and for a CISC processor. Which outcome is most likely?","o":["The RISC machine code has more instructions, as each one does a simpler operation","The RISC machine code has fewer instructions, as each one does more work","Both have the same number of instructions, since they do the same task","The RISC code needs more memory per instruction, as its instructions vary in length"],"a":0,"x":"RISC instructions are simple, so a task needs more of them, and the compiler does more work. CISC has complex instructions that do several steps, so programs are usually shorter in instruction count.","w":["","This swaps the two: fewer, more powerful instructions are a CISC feature.","The instruction set affects the count; the same task needs different numbers of instructions on each.","RISC instructions are fixed length. Variable length is a CISC feature."]}
{"id":"cs-094","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Many smartphones use RISC-based processors such as ARM designs. Which reason best explains this choice?","o":["Complex instructions let one instruction do a whole task, so the battery lasts longer","They need no registers at all, so less memory and power are used","They always run at higher clock speeds than CISC processors","Simpler instructions need simpler circuitry, so less power is used and less heat produced"],"a":3,"x":"RISC designs use simple instructions and simpler hardware, giving lower power use and less heat, which suits battery-powered devices. Complex instructions are a CISC trait, and RISC usually has more registers.","w":["Complex single instructions describe CISC, and they need more complex, power-hungry decoding.","RISC typically has more registers, not none; every processor needs registers.","Clock speed is not guaranteed higher; RISC's advantage here is power efficiency, not speed.",""]}
{"id":"cs-095","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A program takes 80 s on one core. 60 s of that can be shared perfectly across any number of cores; the other 20 s must run in sequence. How long will it take on 4 cores?","o":["20 s","35 s","65 s","50 s"],"a":1,"x":"Only the parallel part speeds up: 60 ÷ 4 = 15 s. The sequential 20 s stays the same. Total 35 s. Dividing the whole 80 s by 4 wrongly assumes everything can be parallelised.","w":["This is 80 ÷ 4, wrongly assuming the whole program speeds up with 4 cores.","","This speeds up the sequential part (20 ÷ 4 = 5) instead of the parallel part: 60 + 5.","This divides the parallel part by 2 instead of 4: 20 + 30, using the wrong number of cores."]}
{"id":"cs-096","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about ROM in a typical computer is correct?","o":["It holds the programs and data currently being worked on","It is volatile, so its contents are lost when power is removed","It is non-volatile and holds start-up instructions such as the bootstrap","It acts as an extension of RAM by using space on the hard disk"],"a":2,"x":"ROM is non-volatile, so it keeps essential start-up instructions (the bootstrap) with the power off. Holding running programs describes RAM. Using disk space as extra memory is virtual memory.","w":["This describes RAM, which holds running programs and data; ROM is mainly read-only start-up code.","ROM is non-volatile; volatility is the feature of RAM, so this confuses the two.","","Using disk space to extend RAM is virtual memory, not ROM."]}
{"id":"cs-097","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A film crew records large video files in a remote area with no mobile signal. Why is virtual (cloud) storage unsuitable as their main store of footage while on location?","o":["Without a network connection footage cannot be uploaded or accessed","Data in virtual storage is volatile and is lost when the camera is switched off","Virtual storage can only be read by one device, so footage cannot be shared","Virtual storage uses flash memory that wears out after only a few uploads"],"a":0,"x":"Virtual storage depends on a network connection and bandwidth. With no signal, footage cannot be uploaded or accessed. It is not volatile; the remote servers use ordinary non-volatile storage and are built for sharing.","w":["","Cloud data is held on non-volatile servers; calling it volatile confuses virtual storage with RAM.","Sharing between many devices is a strength of cloud storage, so this reverses its main advantage.","Flash wear is not a limit users face with cloud storage, and providers manage and replace drives themselves."]}
{"id":"cs-098","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A firm must archive 40 TB of rarely accessed records for many years at the lowest cost per GB. Access speed is unimportant. Which choice is most suitable?","o":["RAM, because it has the fastest access time","SSDs, because flash is the cheapest storage per GB at large capacities","Optical discs, because each disc holds more data than a hard disk","Magnetic disks or tape, because they give very large capacity at low cost per GB"],"a":3,"x":"For bulk archiving where speed does not matter, magnetic media give the lowest cost per GB at huge capacity. RAM is volatile and costly. SSDs cost more per GB. Optical discs hold far less each.","w":["RAM is volatile and very expensive per GB, so it cannot archive data; speed is irrelevant here.","SSDs are usually more expensive per GB than magnetic storage, so they are not the cheapest at scale.","A single optical disc holds far less than a hard disk, so 40 TB would need thousands of discs.",""]}
{"id":"cs-099","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A warehouse must count the contents of a sealed pallet of boxes without opening them or lining up each label. Which input device is most suitable?","o":["Barcode scanner, because it reads many labels at once from any angle","RFID reader, because it can read many tags without line of sight","Magnetic stripe reader, because it reads tags through packaging","Digital camera, because it can read labels on hidden boxes"],"a":1,"x":"RFID tags respond to radio waves, so a reader can detect many tags at once without line of sight, even inside packaging. Barcode scanners need a clear view of each label, usually one at a time.","w":["Barcode scanners need line of sight and normally read one code at a time, so they cannot read hidden boxes.","","Magnetic stripe readers need the card swiped through or placed close to the reader, so this is wrong for sealed pallets.","A camera cannot see labels on boxes that are hidden inside the pallet."]}
{"id":"cs-100","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A 512 GB SSD costs £64. A 2 TB magnetic hard disk costs £48. Taking 1 TB = 1000 GB, about how many times more expensive per GB is the SSD?","o":["About 1.3 times","About 3.9 times","About 5.2 times","About 0.19 times"],"a":2,"x":"SSD: £64 ÷ 512 = £0.125 per GB. HDD: £48 ÷ 2000 = £0.024 per GB. The ratio is 0.125 ÷ 0.024 ≈ 5.2. The trap is comparing only prices or only capacities.","w":["This compares only the prices (64 ÷ 48), ignoring that the capacities differ.","This is the capacity ratio (2000 ÷ 512) only, ignoring the prices.","","This is the ratio the wrong way round, giving how much the HDD costs relative to the SSD."]}
{"id":"cs-101","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"A company is choosing between open source and closed source software for a payroll system. Which is a typical advantage of closed source?","o":["Anyone in the company can modify the code to fix bugs without asking the vendor","The vendor is accountable and normally provides formal support and updates","Users may legally redistribute copies of the program to other organisations","The public can review the source code to find security flaws"],"a":1,"x":"Closed source usually comes with a vendor who takes responsibility, offers support contracts and issues updates. Modifying, redistributing and public code review are features of open source, not closed source.","w":["Modifying the code needs access to the source, which closed source does not give; this is an open source feature.","","Redistribution is normally forbidden by a closed source licence; free copying is an open source feature.","Public code review needs published source code, so this is an open source benefit, not closed source."]}
{"id":"cs-102","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the job of an assembler?","o":["It translates a whole high-level program into object code in a single pass","It translates and runs a high-level program one statement at a time","It translates assembly language mnemonics into machine code, mostly one instruction each","It joins separate object files and library routines into one executable file"],"a":2,"x":"An assembler converts assembly language (mnemonics such as ADD, LDA) into machine code, usually one machine instruction per assembly instruction. Do not confuse it with a compiler, an interpreter or a linker.","w":["That describes a compiler, which translates high-level language; assemblers translate low-level assembly language.","That describes an interpreter, which translates and executes high-level statements one at a time.","","That describes a linker, which combines object modules and libraries; it does not translate mnemonics."]}
{"id":"cs-103","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A compiler is given two lines: A: count = 3 @ 2 (the symbol @ is not in the language). B: if x > 5 then then (every word is a valid token). Which stage first reports each error?","o":["A: syntax analysis; B: lexical analysis","A: lexical analysis; B: code generation","A: syntax analysis; B: syntax analysis","A: lexical analysis; B: syntax analysis"],"a":3,"x":"An invalid symbol cannot be turned into a token, so lexical analysis rejects it. In B every token is valid, but their order breaks the grammar, so syntax analysis catches it. Swapping the stages is the trap.","w":["Reversed: an invalid character fails at tokenising, and a bad token order is a grammar error, not a lexical one.","Code generation happens after the analysis stages; a grammar error in B is found earlier, at syntax analysis.","A is wrong: @ cannot be made into a valid token, so it fails at lexical analysis before syntax is checked.",""]}
{"id":"cs-104","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Three programs each need the same 800 KB library. Each program is 200 KB without it. How much less total storage is used if all three link the library dynamically rather than statically?","o":["1600 KB","800 KB","2400 KB","1400 KB"],"a":0,"x":"Static: 3 × (200 + 800) = 3000 KB. Dynamic: 3 × 200 + one shared 800 = 1400 KB. The saving is 3000 − 1400 = 1600 KB. The trap is to forget one shared copy still needs storing.","w":["","This counts only one library copy saved; dynamic linking removes two of the three copies, saving 1600 KB.","This is 3 × 800 and forgets that dynamic linking still stores one shared copy of the library.","This is the total for dynamic linking, not the saving compared with static linking."]}
{"id":"cs-105","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which project is best suited to the waterfall model?","o":["A game whose features will be shaped by player feedback during development","A prototype app for a client who is not sure what they want","Control software for a medical device with a fixed, regulated specification","A website redesign where the client expects to change priorities every month"],"a":2,"x":"Waterfall works when requirements are clear, stable and documented up front, as with regulated devices. The other projects expect requirements to change. Waterfall handles change badly because each stage is completed once.","w":["Feedback-driven features suggest changing requirements, which suit an agile approach, not waterfall's one-pass stages.","An unsure client means requirements are unclear; prototyping approaches such as RAD suit this, not waterfall.","","Priorities changing monthly need iterations that can absorb change, as in agile; waterfall cannot go back cheaply."]}
{"id":"cs-106","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is a disadvantage of the spiral model compared with other methodologies?","o":["It is costly and needs risk expertise, so suits small projects poorly","Customers see no working software until the final loop is completed","Requirements must be fixed and signed off before any development begins","It needs two developers to share one computer throughout the project"],"a":0,"x":"Each spiral loop includes risk analysis and evaluation, which adds time, cost and specialist skill. That overhead is hard to justify on small, low-risk projects. The other options describe waterfall or extreme programming.","w":["","Spiral builds prototypes or increments on every loop, so users see something early; late delivery is a waterfall trait.","Fixed, signed-off requirements describe waterfall; the spiral expects requirements to develop over successive loops.","Pair programming belongs to extreme programming, not the spiral model."]}
{"id":"cs-107","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Trace this algorithm. What is the final value of total?","code":"total = 0\nn = 0\nwhile n < 5\n    n = n + 2\n    if n MOD 4 != 0 then\n        total = total + n\n    endif\nendwhile","o":["4","8","6","12"],"a":1,"x":"n takes the values 2, 4 and 6. Only 2 and 6 are not multiples of 4, so total = 2 + 6 = 8. The loop stops once n is 6, because 6 < 5 is false. The trap is misreading the condition.","w":["This adds only n = 4, which is a multiple of 4; the condition adds the values that are not multiples of 4.","","This is the final value of n, not total; total is the sum of 2 and 6.","This adds every value of n (2 + 4 + 6) and ignores the MOD 4 condition."]}
{"id":"cs-108","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":3,"q":"Follow this algorithm carefully. What does the list a hold when it finishes?","code":"a = [5, 2, 4, 1, 3]\nfor pass = 1 to 2\n    for i = 0 to 3\n        if a[i] > a[i+1] then\n            swap a[i] and a[i+1]\n        endif\n    next i\nnext pass","o":["[2, 4, 1, 3, 5]","[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]","[2, 1, 4, 3, 5]","[2, 1, 3, 4, 5]"],"a":3,"x":"Pass 1 gives [2,4,1,3,5]. Pass 2 compares 2,4 (no swap), swaps 4,1 giving [2,1,4,3,5], then swaps 4,3 giving [2,1,3,4,5]. The last pair, 4,5, needs no swap. Two passes do not fully sort this list.","w":["This is the list after only the first pass; the second pass still makes two swaps.","Assumes the list ends fully sorted, but two passes are not enough here.","This is part way through pass 2, after the first swap; the swap of 4 and 3 is still to come.",""]}
{"id":"cs-109","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"The index register holds 3. Memory holds 200: 14, 201: 9, 202: 21, 203: 35, 204: 6. LDA 200 is executed using indexed addressing. What value is loaded into the ACC?","o":["14","6","35","203"],"a":2,"x":"Indexed addressing adds the index register to the operand to give the effective address: 200 + 3 = 203. The value stored at 203 is 35. The trap is using the operand 200 alone, which is direct addressing.","w":["Ignores the index register and loads from 200, which is direct addressing.","Off by one: counted the offset as 4 locations, giving address 204 instead of 203.","","Gives the effective address itself, not the value stored there."]}
{"id":"cs-110","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"The Little Man Computer program shown is run and the number 3 is entered. What is output?","code":"       INP\n       STA n\nloop   LDA n\n       BRZ end\n       OUT\n       SUB one\n       STA n\n       BRA loop\nend    HLT\nn      DAT\none    DAT 1","o":["2 1 0","1 2 3","3 2 1 0","3 2 1"],"a":3,"x":"Each pass loads n, tests for zero, outputs it, then subtracts 1. So 3, 2 and 1 are output. When n is 0, BRZ jumps to end before OUT runs, so 0 is never output.","w":["Assumes n is decremented before OUT; SUB comes after OUT, so the first output is 3.","Reverses the order; the loop counts down, so the first value output is 3.","Forgets BRZ sits before OUT, so when n reaches 0 the program halts without printing it.",""]}
{"id":"cs-111","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Alice uses asymmetric encryption to send Bob a message that only Bob can read. Which key should Alice use to encrypt the message?","o":["Alice's private key","Alice's public key","Bob's public key","Bob's private key"],"a":2,"x":"The message is encrypted with the recipient's public key, so only the matching private key, which Bob keeps secret, can decrypt it. Using your own private key is signing, not keeping a message confidential.","w":["Anyone holding Alice's public key could decrypt it, so it is not confidential; this confuses encryption with digital signing.","Bob does not hold Alice's private key, so he could not decrypt it; the sender's own keys are the wrong pair.","","Bob's private key is never shared with Alice; it is the decryption key, not the one used to encrypt."]}
{"id":"cs-112","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Dictionary coding is used on TO BE OR NOT TO BE (spaces not stored). The dictionary holds TO, BE, OR, NOT, each letter 8 bits, and each word in the text is a 2-bit index. Total bits?","o":["84","12","96","104"],"a":0,"x":"The dictionary costs (2+2+2+3) letters × 8 = 72 bits. Six word indexes at 2 bits cost 12 bits. Total 84 bits, against 104 bits for the 13 letters uncompressed. Forgetting to count the dictionary is the common trap.","w":["","Only counts the 6 indexes and ignores the dictionary, which must be stored too.","Uses 4-bit indexes, but only 4 entries need just 2 bits each.","That is the uncompressed size, 13 letters × 8 bits, with no compression applied."]}
{"id":"cs-113","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":3,"q":"10 staff must be able to send confidential messages to each other. With symmetric encryption, each pair of staff shares its own secret key. How many keys are needed, compared with asymmetric?","o":["90 symmetric keys; 10 key pairs","10 symmetric keys; 10 key pairs","45 symmetric keys; 45 key pairs","45 symmetric keys; 10 key pairs"],"a":3,"x":"Each pair needs one shared key: 10 × 9 ÷ 2 = 45. Asymmetric needs only one public/private pair per person, so 10 pairs. The trap is counting each direction separately or one key per person.","w":["Counts 10 × 9, one key per direction; a shared symmetric key works both ways, so 45.","Assumes one symmetric key per person; each pair of people needs its own shared key.","Wrongly assumes asymmetric also needs a pair per pair of people; each person needs just one key pair.",""]}
{"id":"cs-114","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Many students can join many clubs, and each club has many students. How should this many-to-many relationship be implemented in a relational database?","o":["Add a ClubID foreign key field to the Student table","A new link table with StudentID and ClubID as its key","Store a list of all ClubIDs in one field in the Student table","Add a StudentID foreign key field to the Club table"],"a":1,"x":"A many-to-many relationship is resolved with a link table. Its composite key (StudentID, ClubID) holds two foreign keys, giving two one-to-many relationships. A single foreign key on either side allows only one-to-many.","w":["A single ClubID in Student lets each student join only one club, giving one-to-many.","","A list in one field is not atomic, so it breaks 1NF and makes queries awkward.","A single StudentID in Club lets each club have only one student, giving one-to-many the other way."]}
{"id":"cs-115","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Film has the rows (Title, Year): Star Run 2012; Sea Lion 2008; Spark 2015; Stone 2019; Slam 2011; Sharp 2009; Sail 2014. How many rows does WHERE Title LIKE 'S_a%' AND Year > 2010 return?","o":["4","7","3","5"],"a":2,"x":"'_' matches exactly one character, so the third letter must be 'a'. That gives Star Run, Sea Lion, Spark, Slam and Sharp. AND with Year > 2010 leaves Star Run, Spark and Slam. Treating '_' like '%' wrongly includes Sail.","w":["This treats '_' like '%', so Sail is wrongly included; '_' matches exactly one character.","This is what OR would return, since OR gives rows meeting either condition; the query uses AND.","","This applies only the LIKE pattern and ignores Year > 2010, which AND also requires."]}
{"id":"cs-116","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A school LAN uses a star topology. The central hub is replaced with a switch. What is the main benefit of using the switch?","o":["It encrypts each frame so other devices on the LAN cannot read it","It sends each frame only to the port of the destination MAC address","It reads IP addresses to route packets onto other networks","It translates domain names into IP addresses for each device"],"a":1,"x":"A switch learns which MAC address is on each port and forwards a frame only there. A hub repeats every frame to all ports. This cuts collisions and unnecessary traffic. Routing between networks is a router's job.","w":["A switch does not encrypt frames; its forwarding is selective but the data is not protected.","","Routing between networks by IP address is done by a router, not a switch.","Translating domain names to IP addresses is done by DNS, not a switch."]}
{"id":"cs-117","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A student reads the same mailbox on a phone and a laptop. Read and deleted status must stay in step on both. Which protocol should the email clients use to retrieve mail?","o":["SMTP, because it moves mail between devices and servers","POP3, because it keeps every message stored on the mail server","FTP, because it transfers files between a client and a server","IMAP, because messages and their status stay on the server"],"a":3,"x":"IMAP keeps messages on the server, so every device sees the same folders and read status. POP3 usually downloads and removes mail from the server. SMTP is for sending mail, a common trap.","w":["SMTP sends outgoing mail between servers; it is not used to retrieve and synchronise a mailbox.","POP3 normally downloads mail to one device and deletes it from the server, so devices fall out of step.","FTP transfers files, not email; it has no mailbox or read-status features.",""]}
{"id":"cs-118","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":1,"q":"What is the job of a search engine's web crawler (spider)?","o":["To rank pages by counting how many users visit each one","To run the user's search query against the stored pages","To follow hyperlinks from page to page and fetch content for indexing","To convert HTML into the displayed page in the browser"],"a":2,"x":"A crawler follows links from page to page, fetching each page's content so it can be added to the index. Ranking and query matching happen later, using the index, and rendering is the browser's job.","w":["Ranking uses algorithms such as PageRank on links, not a crawler counting visits; this confuses ranking with crawling.","Matching the query is done against the index after crawling, not by the crawler itself.","","Displaying HTML is done by the browser's rendering engine, not a crawler; this confuses a crawler with a browser."]}
{"id":"cs-119","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A server-side script inserts the current price from a database into a web page. What does the user see by choosing 'view page source' in their browser?","o":["The HTML that was sent, with the price in it but none of the server code","The server-side script, including its database query","The HTML template with a placeholder where the price will go","Nothing, because the source of dynamic pages is hidden"],"a":0,"x":"Server-side code runs on the server, and only its output, plain HTML, is sent to the browser. The user never sees the script or the query. The trap is thinking the browser receives the server's code.","w":["","Server-side scripts run on the server and are never sent to the browser, so the query is not visible.","The script has already filled in the price before the page is sent, so no placeholder remains.","Page source is viewable, because the browser must receive the HTML to display it."]}
{"id":"cs-121","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":3,"q":"A search index lists: red → pages {1,2,4,5}; car → {1,3,4,6}; bus → {2,5,6}; blue → {4,5}. Which pages match: red AND (car OR bus) AND NOT blue?","o":["{1}","{2,5}","{1,2,4,5}","{1,2}"],"a":3,"x":"car OR bus gives {1,2,3,4,5,6}. Intersecting with red gives {1,2,4,5}. Removing the blue pages 4 and 5 leaves {1,2}. The traps are treating the bracket as car only or forgetting NOT.","w":["This ignores the bus option, treating the query as red AND car AND NOT blue; the bracket means car OR bus.","This is red AND bus only; it ignores car and never applies NOT blue, which would remove page 5.","This stops before applying NOT blue; pages 4 and 5 contain blue and must be removed.",""]}
{"id":"cs-122","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A binary tree has root 5. Node 5 has left child 2 and right child 9. Node 2 has children 1 (left) and 4 (right). Node 9 has only a left child, 7. What is the post-order traversal?","o":["1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 9","1, 4, 2, 7, 9, 5","5, 2, 1, 4, 9, 7","4, 1, 2, 7, 9, 5"],"a":1,"x":"Post-order visits left subtree, right subtree, then the node. Left subtree gives 1, 4, 2. Right subtree gives 7, 9. The root comes last. The trap is confusing it with in-order or pre-order.","w":["This is in-order (left, node, right), which gives sorted output for a BST, not post-order.","","This is pre-order (node, left, right); the root is visited first rather than last.","Swaps the order within node 2's subtree; post-order visits the left child 1 before the right child 4."]}
{"id":"cs-123","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A linked list is held in arrays. Start pointer = 1. Index: data, next. 0: D, 3. 1: A, 4. 2: E, -1. 3: T, 2. 4: B, 0. The list is A, B, D, T, E. Which change removes D and only D?","o":["Set the next pointer of index 0 to 2","Set the next pointer of index 1 to 3","Set the next pointer of index 0 to -1","Set the next pointer of index 4 to 3"],"a":3,"x":"D is at index 0 and the node before it, B, is at index 4. B's next pointer must skip D and point to D's successor, index 3. Changing D's own pointer leaves D still linked.","w":["This changes D's own pointer, so D stays in the list and T is skipped instead.","This makes A point to T, so B is removed as well as D; the previous node is B.","This changes D's own pointer and cuts off T and E, but D is still reachable from B.",""]}
{"id":"cs-124","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a power given to public authorities by the Regulation of Investigatory Powers Act 2000?","o":["Requiring any company to delete personal data on request","Intercepting communications under a warrant and demanding encryption keys","Fining people who install software without a licence","Prosecuting anyone who gains unauthorised access to a computer"],"a":1,"x":"RIPA lets authorities such as the police and security services intercept communications and demand decryption keys, with proper authorisation. Data deletion, licensing and hacking are covered by other Acts.","w":["Deletion on request is the right to erasure under data protection law, not RIPA.","","Unlicensed software is a copyright matter under the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act, not RIPA.","Unauthorised access is an offence under the Computer Misuse Act, not RIPA."]}
{"id":"cs-125","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A school buys a single-user licence for photo-editing software, then installs it on 20 of its own computers. Which Act has been broken?","o":["Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Data Protection Act 2018","Regulation of Investigatory Powers Act 2000"],"a":0,"x":"Installing software on more machines than the licence allows is unlicensed copying, which breaches copyright law. The school owns the computers, so there is no unauthorised access, personal data or interception involved.","w":["","The Computer Misuse Act needs unauthorised access to a system; the school is using its own machines.","No personal data is being mishandled here, so the Data Protection Act is not the issue.","RIPA covers surveillance and interception, which plays no part in this licensing breach."]}
{"id":"cs-126","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":1,"q":"A customer sees that a company's database holds their old address and asks for it to be corrected. Which data subject right are they using?","o":["The right to erasure","The right of access","The right to object to direct marketing","The right to rectification"],"a":3,"x":"The right to rectification lets people have inaccurate personal data corrected. Erasure would delete the data, and access would only give them a copy of it.","w":["Erasure means deleting the data altogether; the customer wants it corrected, not removed.","Access means seeing a copy of the data held; it does not itself force a correction.","Objecting to marketing stops promotional use; it does not fix wrong data.",""]}
{"id":"cs-127","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":3,"q":"A UK shop's sign-up form has a pre-ticked box reading 'Send me offers'. A customer ignores it, and the shop later sells their email address to an advertiser. Which statement is correct?","o":["Consent is valid because the customer could have unticked the box","Consent is valid provided the shop stores the email address securely","Consent is not valid, as a pre-ticked box is not a clear positive action","Only the sale breaks the law; the shop's own offers are automatically fine"],"a":2,"x":"Under UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018, consent must be a clear, unambiguous positive action. Leaving a pre-ticked box is not, so consent fails. The trap is thinking secure storage or a chance to opt out makes it valid.","w":["Leaving a pre-ticked box is inaction, and silence or inaction does not count as consent.","Security is a separate principle; storing data safely does not make invalid consent valid.","","The shop's own marketing relied on the same invalid consent, so it is not automatically lawful either."]}
{"id":"cs-128","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is a direct environmental harm caused by the short replacement cycle of smartphones and laptops?","o":["Higher broadband prices as old devices overload networks","Less mining of raw materials, because old devices are recycled automatically","Toxic metals like lead and mercury leaching from dumped devices into soil","Lower overall carbon emissions, because new devices are always more efficient"],"a":2,"x":"E-waste often contains hazardous substances that contaminate land and water when dumped. Making new devices also uses energy and raw materials, so frequent replacement is rarely greener overall.","w":["Network prices are not an environmental harm, and old devices do not overload networks.","Recycling rates are low and not automatic, and new devices still need mined materials.","","Ignores the carbon and resources used in manufacture; efficiency gains rarely offset replacing devices early."]}
{"id":"cs-129","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A self-driving car's software must choose between two unavoidable collisions, each harming a different group. Which ethical issue does this mainly raise?","o":["Who decides how the software values lives, and who is accountable","Whether self-driving cars will reduce the number of driving-related jobs","Whether the car's software can be protected by copyright","Whether the manufacturer may legally collect the car's location data"],"a":0,"x":"The dilemma is about programming moral choices into a machine and deciding who is responsible: programmer, manufacturer or owner. Job losses, copyright and location privacy are real issues but different ones.","w":["","Automation and job losses is a genuine issue, but it is not the one raised by a crash decision.","Copyright concerns who owns the code, not who is responsible for its decisions.","Location tracking is a privacy issue, not a dilemma about the software's crash choices."]}
{"id":"cs-130","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A firm secretly installs software that logs every keystroke and takes screenshots of staff computers, without telling staff. Which UK data protection principle is most clearly breached?","o":["Staff data must be stored on servers inside the workplace","Staff must be given a copy of the monitoring software's source code","Employers may not access their own computers without a warrant","Personal data must be processed lawfully, fairly and transparently"],"a":3,"x":"Data protection law requires people to know how their personal data is collected and used. Secret monitoring fails transparency. Employers can monitor if it is justified and disclosed; the trap is thinking monitoring is simply banned.","w":["No principle requires data to be stored on-site; location rules concern transfers abroad, not this case.","Data protection law gives no right to the monitoring software's source code.","No such rule exists: employers own their systems and need no warrant to access them.",""]}
{"id":"cs-131","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A council uses live facial recognition on town-centre cameras to spot wanted criminals. Which is the strongest privacy objection?","o":["Only people with criminal records have their faces scanned, which stigmatises them","Every passer-by is scanned and their biometric data processed, mostly without consent","Anyone may reuse the footage freely because the cameras are on public property","Faces are protected by the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act, so scanning is illegal"],"a":1,"x":"Live facial recognition must scan everyone in view to find a match, so innocent people's biometric data is processed without meaningful consent. The trap is assuming only suspects are scanned.","w":["Wrong: the system has to scan all faces to find matches, not just known offenders.","","Public location does not make footage free to reuse; data protection law still applies.","Copyright protects creative works, not a person's face, so this law is irrelevant."]}
{"id":"cs-132","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":3,"q":"A data centre has 200 servers, each drawing 500 W continuously for 365 days. Cooling adds 40% to the energy used. At 0.25 kg CO₂ per kWh, what are the annual emissions?","o":["219.0 tonnes","525.6 tonnes","306.6 tonnes","306,600 tonnes"],"a":2,"x":"Servers: 200 × 0.5 kW = 100 kW; × 8760 h = 876,000 kWh. Cooling ×1.4 = 1,226,400 kWh. × 0.25 = 306,600 kg = 306.6 tonnes. Traps are omitting cooling or confusing kg with tonnes.","w":["This leaves out the 40% cooling energy, using only the servers' 876,000 kWh.","This adds 140% for cooling (×2.4) instead of 40% (×1.4).","","This gives the figure in kg but labels it tonnes; divide by 1000."]}
{"id":"cs-133","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Why do programmers use abstraction when designing a solution to a complex real-world problem?","o":["To make the finished program run faster by removing loops","To hide unnecessary detail so they can concentrate on the features that matter","To make sure the model reproduces every aspect of the real situation","To split the problem into smaller sub-problems that are solved separately"],"a":1,"x":"Abstraction reduces complexity by leaving out detail that does not affect the problem. Splitting a problem into parts is decomposition, a different technique. Abstraction is about simplifying the problem, not about execution speed.","w":["Abstraction simplifies the problem being modelled; it does not speed up execution. Confusing it with optimisation is the slip.","","This is the opposite of abstraction, which deliberately leaves out detail. The slip is thinking a good model must copy reality exactly.","This describes decomposition, not abstraction. Confusing the two techniques leads to this answer."]}
{"id":"cs-134","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A program simulates a dense metal ball dropped from a height of 2 m. It uses only gravity and ignores air resistance. Which statement about this model is correct?","o":["It is invalid, because an abstraction must include every variable that affects the real drop","It is a decomposition, because each force is solved in a separate module","It will always match a real drop exactly, because gravity is the only important force","Its results may differ slightly from a real drop, because air resistance has been left out"],"a":3,"x":"An abstraction deliberately omits some detail, so it only approximates reality. Here air resistance is tiny but not zero, so results can differ slightly. Leaving detail out is the purpose of abstraction, not a flaw.","w":["Abstractions are meant to leave out detail. Believing a model must include every variable misunderstands what abstraction is.","Decomposition means splitting a problem into parts. Ignoring one force is abstraction, not decomposition.","Omitting detail means the model is only an approximation. Assuming it matches reality exactly is the misconception.",""]}
{"id":"cs-135","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A library system stores books, DVDs and magazines, each with a title and an ID number. The designer creates a general 'item' type holding these shared fields. What is this an example of?","o":["Abstraction by generalisation, grouping things by shared features","Decomposition, breaking the system into smaller modules","Pre-computation, storing results in advance to save time","Concurrency, running several tasks at the same time"],"a":0,"x":"Spotting features that different things share, and treating them as one general category, is abstraction by generalisation. It is not decomposition, because nothing is being split into sub-problems.","w":["","Decomposition splits one problem into parts. Here separate things are being grouped, so the slip is mixing up grouping with splitting.","Pre-computation means calculating results before they are needed. Nothing is being calculated in advance here.","Concurrency is about tasks running together. Defining a shared item type has nothing to do with running tasks."]}
{"id":"cs-136","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A taxi app calculates fare = £3 + £2 per km + £0.30 per minute spent waiting. Which set of data is the minimum needed in an abstract model of the fare?","o":["Distance only","Distance, waiting minutes and car colour","Distance and waiting minutes","Distance, waiting minutes, driver's name and number of passengers"],"a":2,"x":"The fare depends only on distance and waiting time, so the model needs just those two values. Distance alone is too little detail, and car colour, driver name or passenger count add detail that does not affect the fare.","w":["This leaves out waiting time, which changes the fare. Abstracting away too much gives an inaccurate model.","Car colour does not affect the fare. Including irrelevant detail is the slip, because abstraction should remove it.","","Driver's name and passenger count do not affect the fare. Keeping irrelevant detail defeats the point of abstraction."]}
{"id":"cs-137","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Exams are modelled as a graph. Each exam is a node, and an edge joins two exams if at least one student sits both. Which question can NOT be answered from this model alone?","o":["Whether exams A and B can safely be put in the same time slot","Which exams must be in different slots from exam C","Whether any student sits both exam A and exam D","How many students sit both exam A and exam D"],"a":3,"x":"An edge only records whether any student sits both exams, so the number of students is abstracted away. Clashes, and which exams need different slots, can be read directly from the edges.","w":["No edge between A and B means no student sits both, so they can share a slot. The model does answer this.","Exams joined to C by an edge must be in different slots. The edges give this directly.","An edge between A and D shows at least one student sits both. The model does answer this.",""]}
{"id":"cs-138","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which statement best describes caching in a program?","o":["Splitting a large problem into smaller subroutines that can be tested separately","Keeping data that was already fetched or calculated so it can be reused without repeating the work","Checking that every input is valid before it is processed","Encrypting stored data so that other programs cannot read it"],"a":1,"x":"Caching stores the result of earlier work, such as a downloaded file or a calculated value. Later requests can then use the stored copy instead of repeating the work. The other options describe decomposition, validation and encryption.","w":["This describes decomposition or modularity, not caching.","","This describes input validation, which is a different idea from storing results for reuse.","This confuses caching with encryption. Caching is about speed, not security."]}
{"id":"cs-139","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A team builds a new program using a well-tested library for sorting. What is the main benefit of using this reusable component?","o":["It always runs faster than a routine written specially for the task","The new program needs no testing because the library is already tested","The library's inputs and preconditions no longer need to be considered","It saves development time and reduces errors, as the code has already been tested"],"a":3,"x":"Reusable components save time and bring fewer bugs because they have already been used and tested. The trap is thinking this removes the need to test the whole program or to meet the component's preconditions.","w":["A general library can be slower than tailored code, so speed is not guaranteed.","The component still has to be tested within the new program, so integration testing is still needed.","Preconditions still apply, because the library only works if given suitable input.",""]}
{"id":"cs-140","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A weather app caches the forecast for a town for one hour after downloading it. Which is a drawback of this design?","o":["The app must download the forecast again every time the user opens it","The app makes more network requests than it would without a cache","The user may see an out-of-date forecast if it changes within the hour","Reading the cached forecast is slower than downloading a new one"],"a":2,"x":"Cached data can become stale. If the real forecast changes before the hour is up, the app still shows the old copy. Caching reduces requests and is usually faster than downloading again.","w":["This is the behaviour without a cache. Caching avoids repeated downloads within the hour.","Caching reduces network requests, so this reverses its effect.","","Local storage is normally much faster than a network download, so this is the wrong way round."]}
{"id":"cs-141","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":2,"q":"The function getItem(list, n) returns list[n], where the list has length L and indexing starts at 0. Which precondition on n makes sure the call is safe?","o":["$0 \\le n < L$","$1 \\le n \\le L$","$0 < n \\le L$","$n < L$"],"a":0,"x":"With zero-based indexing the valid positions are 0 to L−1, so n must satisfy 0 ≤ n < L. The traps are the off-by-one errors that treat indexing as one-based, and forgetting that n cannot be negative.","w":["","This assumes one-based indexing, so it wrongly allows n = L and excludes position 0.","This excludes the first item at index 0 and allows n = L, which is out of range.","This misses the lower bound, so a negative n would still cause an error."]}
{"id":"cs-142","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":3,"q":"The function below uses a cache, which starts empty. How many times in total is fib called, including the first call, when fib(5) is run?","code":"cache = {}\nfunction fib(n)\n  if n in cache then return cache[n]\n  if n <= 1 then return n\n  r = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n  cache[n] = r\n  return r\nendfunction","o":["6","15","9","11"],"a":2,"x":"The calls go fib(5), fib(4), fib(3), fib(2), fib(1), fib(0), then fib(1) from fib(3), then fib(2) from fib(4) as a cache hit, then fib(3) from fib(5) as a cache hit. That is 9. Without the cache it would be 15.","w":["This counts only n+1 calls and forgets the extra calls for fib(1) and the cache hits, which are still calls.","This is the number of calls without caching, so it ignores the cache.","","This overcounts. The cache hits for fib(2) and fib(3) stop those calls from making further calls."]}
{"id":"cs-143","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":1,"q":"A program checks a postcode in five different places. Why is it better to write one sub-procedure and call it each time, rather than copying the code five times?","o":["The program runs noticeably faster because the code is only stored once","The sub-procedure does not need to be tested separately from the main program","A change or bug fix is made in one place rather than five","No variables or parameters are needed because the main program shares them"],"a":2,"x":"One sub-procedure means one place to correct or improve, so the copies cannot drift apart. Calling it does not speed up execution, and the sub-procedure should still be tested on its own.","w":["Calling a procedure usually adds a little overhead; the benefit is maintainability, not speed.","Sub-procedures are easier to test in isolation, so testing is not avoided.","","Sub-procedures normally use parameters and local variables; they do not make data passing unnecessary."]}
{"id":"cs-144","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A program reads marks until the sentinel -1 is entered, then outputs the mean. Which loop structure adds up the marks correctly, without adding the sentinel?","o":["Read mark; while mark != -1: add mark to total; read mark","While mark != -1: read mark; add mark to total","Read mark; while mark != -1: read mark; add mark to total","Repeat: read mark; add mark to total; until mark == -1"],"a":0,"x":"Read once before the loop, then add the mark and read the next one at the end of each pass. The test then happens before each addition, so -1 is never added. Reading at the start of the loop body is the trap.","w":["","The loop tests mark before any value has been read, so the first test uses an undefined value.","Reading at the top of the loop discards the first mark and adds the final -1 to the total.","The check comes after the addition, so the sentinel -1 is added to the total."]}
{"id":"cs-145","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"function double(n)\n    return n * 2\nendfunction\nfunction addOne(n)\n    return n + 1\nendfunction\nx = 3\ny = double(addOne(x))\nz = addOne(double(x))\nprint(y - z)","o":["0","1","-1","2"],"a":1,"x":"y = double(addOne(3)) = double(4) = 8. z = addOne(double(3)) = addOne(6) = 7. So y - z = 1. The order of composition matters: the inner call runs first and the two results differ.","w":["Assumed both lines give the same value because they use the same two functions; the order changes the result.","","Subtracted the wrong way round: z - y would be -1, but the program prints y - z.","Forgot the addOne in z, giving 6, so 8 - 6 = 2; z is addOne(6) = 7."]}
{"id":"cs-146","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A game needs a sub-procedure to check whether a password is valid. Which design makes it most reusable and easiest to test on its own?","o":["It asks the user for the password, then prints \"Valid\" or \"Invalid\"","It reads a global variable called pw and prints the result of the check","It takes the password and a username, and asks the user to type them again","It takes the password as a parameter and returns True or False"],"a":3,"x":"A sub-procedure that receives its input as a parameter and returns a result does one job and can be tested with fixed values. Mixing in input, output or globals ties it to one program and makes it harder to reuse.","w":["Mixing user input and output into the check means it cannot be tested with fixed values or reused elsewhere.","Relying on a global variable hides the dependency, and printing instead of returning stops other code using the result.","Asking again for values it was already given duplicates input; the parameters should be used directly.",""]}
{"id":"cs-147","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A program has six steps: A open file, B read records, C count them, D sort them, E display results, F close file. A is before B; B before C, D and F; C and D before E. How many valid orders exist?","o":["4","6","8","2"],"a":2,"x":"A then B must come first. The other four steps C, D, E, F can then be arranged so that E follows both C and D: one third of the 24 arrangements, which is 8. Assuming a single fixed order is the usual trap.","w":["Fixed C before D and then slotted F into 4 places, missing that D before C is also valid.","Counted the 3! orders of C, D and E with no restriction and ignored F.","","Ignored F, which can go anywhere after B, so only the two orders CDE and DCE were counted."]}
{"id":"cs-148","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":1,"q":"Which condition is logically equivalent to NOT (a > 5 AND b < 3)?","o":["a <= 5 AND b >= 3","a < 5 OR b > 3","a <= 5 OR b >= 3","a > 5 OR b < 3"],"a":2,"x":"De Morgan's law: negating an AND gives an OR of the negated parts. NOT(a > 5) is a <= 5 and NOT(b < 3) is b >= 3. The traps are keeping the AND or getting the boundaries wrong.","w":["Negated both parts correctly but kept AND; De Morgan's law requires the AND to change to OR.","Changed AND to OR but negated the comparisons wrongly; NOT(a > 5) is a <= 5, not a < 5.","","Changed AND to OR but forgot to negate the comparisons, so this is not the opposite condition."]}
{"id":"cs-149","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"What is the value of x after this code runs?","code":"x = 12\nif x > 5 then\n    x = x + 3\nendif\nif x > 10 then\n    x = x * 2\nelseif x > 20 then\n    x = 0\nendif","o":["30","24","15","0"],"a":0,"x":"The first if is separate, so x becomes 15. The second if tests the new value: 15 > 10, so x becomes 30. The elseif is skipped because its if branch ran. The trap is using the old x or merging the ifs.","w":["","Used the original x = 12 in the second test and ignored the +3 already applied.","Treated the second if as an elseif of the first, so only one branch ran.","Assumed x > 20 is also checked and runs; an elseif is skipped once the if branch has run."]}
{"id":"cs-150","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"What does this program print?","code":"n = 100\ncount = 0\nwhile n > 10\n    n = n DIV 2\n    count = count + 1\nendwhile\nprint(count)","o":["5","6","3","4"],"a":3,"x":"n goes 100, 50, 25, 12, 6 with count 1 to 4. At n = 6 the test n > 10 is false, so the loop stops. Running until n reaches 1 would give 6, which ignores the loop condition.","w":["Did one extra iteration, halving 6 to 3 even though 6 > 10 is false.","Halved all the way to 1 (6 halvings) instead of stopping when n is no longer above 10.","Stopped one iteration early, when n reached 12, forgetting that 12 > 10 is still true.",""]}
{"id":"cs-151","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":3,"q":"With a = 9, b = 7, c = 0, what is printed? (AND is evaluated before OR.)","code":"if a > b OR b > 5 AND c > 0 then\n    print(\"X\")\nelseif NOT (a > b) AND b MOD 2 == 1 then\n    print(\"Y\")\nelse\n    print(\"Z\")\nendif","o":["Z","X","Y","X then Z"],"a":1,"x":"AND binds tighter than OR: a > b is true, so the whole OR is true whatever the AND gives. X prints and the rest is skipped. Reading it left to right as (a > b OR b > 5) AND c > 0 gives false and leads to Z.","w":["Evaluated left to right as (a > b OR b > 5) AND c > 0, ignoring that AND has higher precedence than OR.","","Ignored the NOT in the second condition; NOT (9 > 7) is false, and the first branch runs anyway.","Thought the else belongs to a separate if so Z also prints; only one branch of an if/elseif/else runs."]}
{"id":"cs-152","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A program must ask for a number from 1 to 10, always ask at least once, and keep asking while the input is invalid. Which structure fits best?","o":["while x >= 1 AND x <= 10, ask for x","for i = 1 to 10, ask for x","repeat, ask for x, until x >= 1 AND x <= 10","if x < 1 OR x > 10 then ask for x again"],"a":2,"x":"A post-condition loop runs its body first and then tests, so the prompt appears at least once and repeats until the input is valid. A while loop with this condition repeats on valid input, and an if asks only once more.","w":["The condition is the wrong way round: it loops while input is valid, and x has no value on first test.","A for loop repeats a fixed 10 times regardless of whether the input is valid.","","A single if gives only one retry, so a second invalid input is accepted; validation needs a loop."]}
{"id":"cs-153","s":"cs","t":"2.1.5","lv":1,"q":"A program must: A load an image from disk, B resize that image (needs the loaded image), C load a sound file. Which statement about running these concurrently is correct?","o":["A and B can run together, because both work on the same image","A and C can run together, but B must wait until A has finished","B and C cannot run together, because loading must finish before any resizing","All three must run in order, because one processor can only do one task"],"a":1,"x":"A and C are independent, so they can overlap. B depends on the output of A, so it must wait. Concurrency means finding tasks with no data dependency between them.","w":["B needs the finished image from A, so they cannot overlap; sharing an image is a dependency, not a reason to run together.","","B depends only on A, not on C, so B and C are independent; this wrongly treats every load as a prerequisite.","Independent tasks need no fixed order, and concurrent tasks can be interleaved on one processor; this confuses concurrency with strict sequencing."]}
{"id":"cs-155","s":"cs","t":"2.1.5","lv":2,"q":"A job takes 60 s on one core. 40 s of it can be split perfectly across cores; the other 20 s must run sequentially. Ignoring overheads, how long does it take on 4 cores?","o":["15 s","50 s","30 s","45 s"],"a":2,"x":"The sequential part stays 20 s. The parallel part takes 40 ÷ 4 = 10 s. Total 30 s. The trap is dividing the whole 60 s by 4, ignoring the sequential part.","w":["This divides the whole 60 s by 4, wrongly assuming every part of the task can be parallelised.","This saves only 10 s, but the parallel 40 s shrinks to 10 s, so 30 s is saved.","","This takes a quarter off the whole 60 s, but the saving is three quarters of the parallel 40 s."]}
{"id":"cs-156","s":"cs","t":"2.1.5","lv":1,"q":"A photo editor applies a slow filter on a separate thread from the one handling the interface. What is the main benefit of this design?","o":["The interface can still respond to the user while the filter runs","The filter is guaranteed to finish faster, even on a single core","The two threads never need to coordinate over shared data","The program uses less memory than a single-threaded version"],"a":0,"x":"Separating the slow work from the interface thread keeps the program responsive. It does not guarantee faster completion on a single core, and shared data still needs synchronisation.","w":["","On a single core the threads share one processor, so the filter is not faster; the gain is responsiveness, not speed.","Threads that share the image data still need locks or other synchronisation to avoid race conditions.","Extra threads need their own stacks and bookkeeping, so memory use usually rises rather than falls."]}
{"id":"cs-157","s":"cs","t":"2.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A pipeline has 3 stages, each taking 2 s per item, with one item per stage at a time. Items pass through the stages in order. How long to process 4 items, versus 24 s with no overlap?","o":["8 s","12 s","14 s","24 s"],"a":1,"x":"The first item takes 6 s to fill the pipeline. After that one item completes every 2 s, so 3 more items add 6 s: 12 s total. The trap is forgetting the fill time.","w":["This is 4 × 2 s, ignoring the 6 s the first item needs to pass through all three stages.","","This adds 4 × 2 s after the first item, counting one extra item; only 3 items remain after the first.","This is the non-overlapped time; the pipeline lets different items occupy different stages at once, so it is quicker."]}
{"id":"cs-158","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the halting problem is correct?","o":["It can be solved, but only on a computer with enough memory","No single algorithm can decide, for every program and input, whether it halts","It is solvable, but the time needed grows exponentially with program size","It only affects programs that contain an infinite loop"],"a":1,"x":"The halting problem is uncomputable: no algorithm works for all programs and inputs. This is different from intractable problems, which are solvable but too slow, and it is not a matter of resources.","w":["Confuses uncomputable with resource-limited; no amount of memory makes a general halting test possible.","","Describes an intractable problem. The halting problem has no algorithm at all, however much time is allowed.","The result is about deciding halting in general; it is not limited to programs that visibly loop forever."]}
{"id":"cs-159","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A binary search looks for a value in a sorted list of 100 items. Each step compares the target with one item. What is the maximum number of comparisons needed?","o":["50","6","8","7"],"a":3,"x":"The number of items left falls 100, 50, 25, 12, 6, 3, 1, so at most 7 comparisons. The trap is using log₂100 ≈ 6.6 rounded down to 6, which forgets the final comparison.","w":["Halves the list once and stops; binary search keeps halving, so far fewer than 50 comparisons are needed.","Rounds log₂100 down and forgets the last comparison on the single remaining item.","Rounds up and then adds one; 7 comparisons already cover up to 127 items.",""]}
{"id":"cs-160","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A depot D and 11 towns are given. A route starts at D, visits every town once and returns to D. Each different ordering of the 11 towns counts as a different route. How many routes are there?","o":["39,916,800","479,001,600","19,958,400","132"],"a":0,"x":"The start is fixed at D, so only the 11 towns are ordered: 11! = 39,916,800. The trap is using 12!. Growth like this makes the problem intractable, so heuristics are used for large cases.","w":["","Uses 12!, treating the depot as a free choice of position even though the start is fixed.","Halves the count as if a reversed route were the same, but the question counts each ordering separately.","Multiplies only 12 × 11 instead of all of 11 × 10 × … × 1; the count grows factorially."]}
{"id":"cs-161","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":3,"q":"The subset-sum search below is run with v = [5, 3, 4, 2]. Including the first call solve(0, 0), how many times is solve called in total?","code":"function solve(i, total)\n    if total == 9 then\n        return True\n    endif\n    if total > 9 or i == 4 then\n        return False\n    endif\n    if solve(i + 1, total + v[i]) then\n        return True\n    endif\n    return solve(i + 1, total)\nendfunction\nsolve(0, 0)","o":["5","7","9","31"],"a":2,"x":"Calls in order: (0,0), (1,5), (2,8), (3,12), (3,8), (4,10), (4,8), (2,5), (3,9). That is 9. The search backtracks from dead ends and stops once 9 is found, so the full tree is not explored.","w":["Counts only calls that make further calls, leaving out those that return straight away as base cases.","Leaves out the two calls that return immediately because the total exceeds 9, but they are still calls.","","Counts the full include/exclude tree, ignoring both the pruning when total exceeds 9 and the early stop."]}
